ticle/f0c09f6d-fdf1-5e38-99de-6f2cef731fe0', 'word_count': 225, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f0c09f6d-fdf1-5e38-99de-6f2cef731fe0'}, {'abstract': 'This week, Kevin Sr. goes a-wandering: What’s a little cultural appropriation when you’ve got a solid messiah complex?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/04/30/arts/television/the-leftovers-recap-crazy-whitefella-thinking.html', 'snippet': 'This week, Kevin Sr. goes a-wandering: What’s a little cultural appropriation when you’ve got a solid messiah complex?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1981, shortly after his wife died, policeman Kevin Garvey Sr. of Mapleton, N.Y., vacationed in Niagara Falls with his son, Kevin Jr. Using the tape recorder his mother gave him before she passed, the younger Garvey recorded conversations with his dad. The two men talked about the parallels between “Taxi Driver” and the assassination attempt on President Ronald Reagan, and about how AAA guidebooks are unreliable because they steer travelers to businesses that have made deals with AAA. Decades later, when 140 million people mysteriously disappeared from the Earth, Kevin Sr. lost his grip on his sanity. But he held onto the tape labeled “Niagara 81,” and he listened to it repeatedly, contemplating its meaning.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/28/watching/30leftovers/28recaps-slide-3294-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/04/28/watching/30leftovers/28recaps-slide-3294-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/28/watching/30leftovers/28recaps-slide-3294-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit':