major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cantor Fitzgerald LP', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-01T07:21:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/77c26722-6254-5a30-bec3-52469f77e97e', 'word_count': 676, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77c26722-6254-5a30-bec3-52469f77e97e'}, {'abstract': 'Of the flurry of policy decisions the White House is grappling with, advancing normalization with Cuba is a no-brainer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/01/opinion/push-and-pull-on-cuba-trump-obama.html', 'snippet': 'Of the flurry of policy decisions the White House is grappling with, advancing normalization with Cuba is a no-brainer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In recent weeks, as the White House has been consumed by loud debates over health care, taxes and trade, there has been another, quieter debate occurring beneath the surface. Government agencies and lawmakers have been pulling the administration in two directions on whether to continue the Obama administration’s path on relations with Cuba.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/01/opinion/01mon2/01mon2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 568, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/01/opinion/01mon2/01mon2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 568}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/01/opinion/01mon2/01mon2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 970, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'image