tion': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ceb060bb-547a-5053-9176-5ad71782f1bc', 'word_count': 335, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ceb060bb-547a-5053-9176-5ad71782f1bc'}, {'abstract': 'What are the hidden benefits of spending time doing something you will never do well?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/02/learning/what-do-you-gain-from-pursuing-something-you-do-really-really-badly.html', 'snippet': 'What are the hidden benefits of spending time doing something you will never do well?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Think about the times you have seen people make complete fools of themselves attempting to do something they are clearly not good at. What did you think, and how did you react?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/29/opinion/29sportingWeb/29sportingWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 390, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/04/29/opinion/29sportingWeb/29sportingWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 390}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/29/opinion/29sportingWeb/29sportingWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 666, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/29/opinion/29sportingWeb/29sportingWeb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1333, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/29/opinion/29sportingWeb/29sportingWeb-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/04/29/opinion/29sportingWeb/29sportingWeb-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/29/opinion/29sportingWeb/29sportingWeb-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Do You Gain From Pursuing Something You Do Really, Really Badly?', 'kicker': 'Student Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reading and Writing Skills (Education)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-02T09:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Learning', 'section_name': 'The Learning Network', 'byline': {'original': 'By Caroline Crosson Gilpin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Caroline', 'middlename': 'Crosson', 'lastname': 'Gilpin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc48de97-a7aa-5dfd-9360-185706d570c5', 'word_count': 568, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc48de97-a7aa-5dfd-9360-185706d570c5'}, {'abstract': 'Like quinoa before it, açaí has become a craze among health-conscious Americans — creating a bonanza in the regions where it’s grown.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/02/magazine/the-superfood-gold-rush.html', 'snippet': 'Like quinoa before it, açaí has become a craze among health-conscious Americans — creating a bonanza in the regions where it’s grown.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the aisles of the American supermarket, hierarchies arise and collapse with the velocity of soap-opera drama. The produce section is especially cutthroat; here, because novel products are so rare, it’s the stories that must change to entice consumers. Back in the 1970s, the Chinese gooseberry went global — cultivated in New Zealand and shipped to the world — after being rechristened as the friendlier kiwi. Just a few years ago, kale was king, rehabbed as a luxury green after decades as a garnish and a Southern standby. Similarly, quinoa, long a staple crop in the Andes, has lately become a Western fixture, an ostensibly more primal alternative to rice. The latest entrant to this contest is Brazilian açaí, a purplish, antioxidant-rich stone fruit — though most call it a berry — foraged from trees in the Amazon River basin.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/07/magazine/07acai/07mag-07acai-t_CA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 443, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/07/magazine/07acai/07mag-07acai-t_CA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 443}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/07/magazine/07acai/07mag-07acai-t_CA1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 755, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/07/magazine/07acai/07mag-07acai-t_CA1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1395, 'width': 1891, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/07/magazine/07acai/07mag-07acai-t_CA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/07/magazine/07acai/07mag-07acai-t_CA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/07/magazine/07acai/07mag-07acai-t_CA1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Superfood Gold Rush', 'kicker': 'The Money Issue', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Superfood Effect', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Foods', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brazil', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fruit', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-02T09:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jamie Lauren Keiles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jamie', 'middlename': 'Lauren', 'lastname': 'Keiles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e451028-8dfb-5dc8-b0f8-977613ade6da', 'word_count': 997, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e451028-8dfb-5dc8-b0f8-977613ade6da'}, {'abstract': 'The rising superpower has built up enormous holdings in poor, resource-rich African countries — but its business partners there aren’t always thrilled.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/02/magazine/is-china-the-worlds-new-colonial-power.html', 'snippet': 'The rising superpower has built up enormous holdings in poor, resource-rich African countries — but its business partners there aren’t always thrilled.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every weekday before dawn, a morning migration takes place near the desert on Africa’s southwestern coast. At 5:30 in the Namibian enclave Swakopmund, whose century-old buildings still bear the imprint of German colonization, solitary men in khaki uniforms emerge from houses and apartment complexes, the white reflective strips on their pants flashing as they walk briskly through the darkness. They are not African but Chinese. No one else is stirring in the Atlantic Coast 