FAMATION LEAGUE', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-02T17:50:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/b0176cfc-fd31-5ebe-a709-e813bba4e985', 'word_count': 165, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b0176cfc-fd31-5ebe-a709-e813bba4e985'}, {'abstract': 'Television writers struggle to maintain a middle-class life. A new contract will help.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/02/opinion/we-write-your-favorite-shows-we-need-to-get-paid.html', 'snippet': 'Television writers struggle to maintain a middle-class life. A new contract will help.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Into the wee hours of this morning, the negotiating committee of my union, the Writers Guild of America — a motley group, but one that includes the creators of some of your favorite movies and TV shows — hammered out a deal with the Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers. One could almost hear the entire city of Los Angeles breathing a sigh of relief. Like many guild members, I voted to authorize a strike, and lobbied others to do the same, although a strike would have been personally devastating. I did so because the alternative would have been worse.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/02/opinion/02groteWeb/02groteWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/02/opinion/02groteWeb/02groteWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/02/opinion/02groteWeb/02groteWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'c