T19:21:37+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Style', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/c5550abd-0f75-570f-aee9-4674eb5b8db3', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/c5550abd-0f75-570f-aee9-4674eb5b8db3'}, {'abstract': 'This is the strongest episode of “The Handmaid’s Tale” yet but it’s held back from greatness by a near-fatal flaw.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/02/arts/television/the-handmaids-tale-episode-3-recap.html', 'snippet': 'This is the strongest episode of “The Handmaid’s Tale” yet but it’s held back from greatness by a near-fatal flaw.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s a curious musical choice nestled in one of Offred’s pivotal memories that illuminates some of my issues so far with “The Handmaid’s Tale.” Offred attends a march with Moira to protest the country’s devolution into a totalitarian state. Women are being fired from their jobs and their bank accounts frozen, with funds being transferred to their husbands or closest male kin. The people at the march are similar to Offred — young, urbane, angry at the shape the world is in now that oppression is hurting them directly. Soon the march turns violent as the protesters are shot at. A down-tempo version of “Heart of Glass” by Blondie slinks through this sequence, which culminates with Offred and Moira taking cover in a vacant cafe. A bomb goes off blowing the windows out and glass cascades down. The song fits neither the mood nor the message of the scene and its placement demonstrates for me how “The Handmaid’s Tale” can swing wildly from blisteringly powerful to absolutely leaden thanks to decisions that feel artless.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/25/arts/25handmaid-03/25handmaid-slide-EWMW-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 's