05-02T20:11:03+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jason Lee and Devon Knight', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jason', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Devon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Knight', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/64675560-c22b-550d-bcb1-1be515befa6c', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/64675560-c22b-550d-bcb1-1be515befa6c'}, {'abstract': 'You don’t have to wait in line in the rain. A rich, fluffy version with a lacy, crisp crust is right at hand.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/02/dining/french-toast-recipe-video.html', 'snippet': 'You don’t have to wait in line in the rain. A rich, fluffy version with a lacy, crisp crust is right at hand.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you’re like me, when it comes to French toast, you’re a slosher, someone who just throws the ingredients together. Your method might go something like this: Slosh milk into bowl (I stock the 2 percent kind), add a couple of eggs and whisk until it looks right. Soak the bread, and sizzle in butter until done. Sometimes it’s delicious, and sometimes it’s disappointing. But it’s rarely worth the $12 price and the hour’s wait, sometimes in the rain, that the mobs sign up for every Sunday at brunch destinations like Egg, in Brooklyn, and Sqirl, in Los Angeles.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Restaurant-Worthy French Toast, Without the Wait', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'French Toast  Worth Staying At Home For', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 