ame': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barbaro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'briefing', '_id': 'nyt://article/54b65112-3400-516b-a232-133de2c54cbe', 'word_count': 483, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54b65112-3400-516b-a232-133de2c54cbe'}, {'abstract': 'Have a better experience at your next doctor’s appointment by being more mindful.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/03/well/mind/how-to-be-mindful-at-the-doctors-office.html', 'snippet': 'Have a better experience at your next doctor’s appointment by being more mindful.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Life presents us with this quandary: When we most need to communicate effectively, we are often filled with anxiety. When we are reactive to anxious states, we tend to shut down, act impulsively or crave solace. None of these responses helps us work with our doctors. To have a better experience — to ask the difficult question, share a moment of vulnerability or discuss symptoms — start by being mindful.” – Lee Rosen, Ph.D., assistant professor of psychiatry at the Larner College of Medicine at the University of Vermont', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/15/well/03mfrl-doctor/03mfrl-doctor-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/15/well/03mfrl-doctor/03mfrl-doctor-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/15/well/03mfrl-doctor/03mfrl-doctor-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/15/well/03