multimedia', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mbari', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mbari', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/c8c2c30b-e0f1-504d-8c6f-e068e5e01a62', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/c8c2c30b-e0f1-504d-8c6f-e068e5e01a62'}, {'abstract': 'Scientists near Monterey Bay in California find that giant larvaceans, a kind of zooplankton, can filter all of the water between 300 and 1,000 feet in the bay in less than two weeks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/03/science/giant-larvaceans-filter-oceans-carbon.html', 'snippet': 'Scientists near Monterey Bay in California find that giant larvaceans, a kind of zooplankton, can filter all of the water between 300 and 1,000 feet in the bay in less than two weeks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Swimming hundreds of feet beneath the ocean’s surface in many parts of the world are prolific architects called giant larvaceans. These zooplankton are not particularly giant themselves (they resemble tadpoles and are about the size of a pinkie finger), but every day, they construct one or more spacious “houses” that can exceed three feet in length.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Disposable Mucus Houses, These Zooplankton Filter the Oceans', 'kicker': 'Trilobites', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cleaning House: Tiny Creatures That Filter the Oceans', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oceans and Seas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Filters', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Science Advances (Journal)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Monterey Bay Aquarium Research Institute', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}],