 a harried, financially imperiled aristocrat.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/03/theater/the-roundabout-review.html', 'snippet': 'This revival of J. B. Priestley’s 1932 comedy depicts a busy Saturday in the life of a harried, financially imperiled aristocrat.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The insatiable American appetite for British drawing-room comedy is well nourished in “The Roundabout,” a revival of J. B. Priestley’s 1932 comedy at 59E59 Theaters. This sparkling, impeccably staged play — produced by the Cahoots Theater Company, the Other Cheek and Park Theater as part of 59E59’s Brits Off Broadway program — will be catnip to “Downton Abbey” devotees, with equal doses of humor and insight.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/04/arts/04ROUNDABOUT/03ROUNDABOUT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/04/arts/04ROUNDABOUT/03ROUNDABOUT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/04/arts/04ROUNDABOUT/03ROUNDABOUT-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/04/arts/04ROUNDABOUT/03ROUNDABOUT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/04/arts/04ROUNDABOUT/03ROUNDABOUT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/04/arts/04ROUNDABOUT/03ROUNDABOUT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/04/arts/04ROUNDABOUT/03ROUNDABOUT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: ‘The Roundabout’ Gathers Nervous Rich People on a Not-So-Lazy Saturday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Nervous Rich People on a Not-So-Lazy Saturday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Roundabout (Play)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Brits Off Broadway Festival', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': '59E59 THEATERS', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Priestley, J B', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ross, Hugh (Theater Director)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cahoots Theater Co', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Other Cheek (Theater Co)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Park Theater', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-03T20:52:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Webster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Webster', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7dc4dad-1e9f-52c6-add1-a3fc77fe0792', 'word_count': 461, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7dc4dad-1e9f-52c6-add1-a3fc77fe0792'}, {'abstract': 'An obscure State Department program made headlines after it appeared to be promoting the president’s Florida getaway. The reality is more complicated — and almost surreal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/03/magazine/in-mar-a-lago-blunder-a-glimpse-at-the-difficulties-of-soft-diplomacy-under-trump.html', 'snippet': 'An obscure State Department program made headlines after it appeared to be promoting the president’s Florida getaway. The reality is more complicated — and almost surreal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the beginning of April, a short blog post about Donald Trump’s Mar-a-Lago resort appeared on ShareAmerica, a web property administered by the State Department. The post was an anodyne history of the property, one of many such posts on the site, which is full of easily digestible tidbits about America meant for a foreign audience (“New York Restaurant Named Best in the World,” for example, or “The Year of the Rooster: How Are Americans Celebrating?”). It sat quietly for several weeks, until a promotional tweet from a State Department account caught the eye of Senator Ron Wyden of Oregon, who wondered aloud on Twitter why the federal government was in the business of advertising the president’s private club. The post was removed after the subsequent furor, an apology left in its place.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/02/magazine/02mag-IIP-1/02mag-IIP-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/02/magazine/02mag-IIP-1/02mag-IIP-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/02/magazine/02mag-IIP-1/02mag-IIP-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/02/magazine/02mag-IIP-1/02mag-IIP-1-artic