607fedffdc', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/604a29d9-4e19-5851-b3d0-e1607fedffdc'}, {'abstract': "The director discusses a sequence from the film that was inspired by Alfred Hitchcock's “North by Northwest.”", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/04/movies/james-gunn-narrates-a-scene-from-guardians-of-the-galaxy-vol-2.html', 'snippet': "The director discusses a sequence from the film that was inspired by Alfred Hitchcock's “North by Northwest.”", 'lead_paragraph': 'One scene in the new “Guardians of the Galaxy” sequel looks a lot like a Hitchcock movie, but with more explosions and spaceships. The director James Gunn took a cue from the crop duster sequence in the thriller “North by Northwest” to stage a battle between Gamora (Zoe Saldana) and her adopted sister Nebula (Karen Gillan). In an interview, Mr. Gunn spoke about why it’s one of the few moments in the film without music and how he was inspired to outdo a “Fast and Furious” movie. Here are excerpts from that conversation.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'James Gunn Narrates a Scene From ‘Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Guardians of the Galaxy Vol 2 (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gunn, James (1970- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Saldana, Zoe', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-04T15:37:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mekado Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mekado', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/82b37d42-2f5e-5491-a315-84fa3f5500bd', 'word_count': 625, 'uri': 'nyt://article/82b37d42-2f5e-5491-a315-84fa3f5500bd'}, {'abstract': 'Suggested reading from critics and editors at The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/04/books/review/10-new-books-we-recommend-this-week.html', 'snippet': 'Suggested reading from critics and editors at The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you loved “My Name Is Lucy Barton,” you’ll welcome Elizabeth Strout’s new work, “Anything Is Possible,” as a homecoming: it takes place in Amgash, Ill., the town of Lucy’s birth, and revisits many of the characters who populated Lucy’s conversations with her mother in Strout’s acclaimed 2016 novel. New collections from Richard Bausch and Deb Olin Unferth push the emotional and technical boundaries of short fiction. A debut memoir in travelogue mode charts the author’s path to sobriety in the Orkney Islands. And Lidia Yuknavitch’s “The Book of Joan” hurls us into a future world wrecked by climate change, in which for Earth’s survivors, storytelling becomes a tool of both tyranny and resistance.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/04/books/review/04edchoice-covers/04edchoice-covers-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 294, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/04/books/review/04edchoice-covers/04edchoice-covers-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 294}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/04/books/review/04edchoice-covers/04edchoice-covers-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 501, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/04/books/review/04edchoice-covers/04edchoi