tical)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-05T00:47:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/d4dcf520-afc5-5984-8075-427e5630cfd9', 'word_count': 454, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d4dcf520-afc5-5984-8075-427e5630cfd9'}, {'abstract': 'Castro isn’t bitter at the Cubs, who jettisoned him for not lengthening at-bats. But it’s the Yankees who now benefit from his free-swinging approach.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/04/sports/baseball/starlin-castro-new-york-yankees-chicago-cubs.html', 'snippet': 'Castro isn’t bitter at the Cubs, who jettisoned him for not lengthening at-bats. But it’s the Yankees who now benefit from his free-swinging approach.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHICAGO — Starlin Castro was fed up as the 2013 season came to a close, and not just because of another last-place finish for the Chicago Cubs, their fourth in a row. No longer was he a beacon of a brighter future. After making the National League All-Star team the previous two seasons — all by the time he had turned 23 — Castro was lost, his head swimming with other people’s ideas about the type of player he should be.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/05/sports/baseball/05yankees02/05yankees02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/05/sports/baseball/05yankees02/05yankees02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/05/sports/baseball/05yankees02/05yankees02-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 15