', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gerry Mullany', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gerry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mullany', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c18a9301-b69f-574e-8384-875e0f95288f', 'word_count': 313, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c18a9301-b69f-574e-8384-875e0f95288f'}, {'abstract': 'Schools should be embarrassed when they stigmatize students whose parents owe money for meals.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/05/opinion/shaming-school-lunch-bills.html', 'snippet': 'Schools should be embarrassed when they stigmatize students whose parents owe money for meals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The humiliation inflicted on children whose parents are late paying school lunch bills — or are too poor to pay them at all — is a national disgrace. Cafeteria workers berate the children for being unable to pay, rather than allowing them to eat, or stigmatize them by stamping their arms with messages like “I need lunch money.” An article in The Times on Monday recounted the painful experience of one student whose meal was dumped in the garbage.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/05/opinion/05fri3web/05fri3web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/05/opinion/05fri3web/05fri3web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/05/opinion/05fri3web/05fri3web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge'