'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Biarritz (France)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-05T14:45:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Insider', 'section_name': 'Times Insider', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alissa J. Rubin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alissa', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Rubin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/838a4413-6b50-5400-86e1-37730b81c84b', 'word_count': 1193, 'uri': 'nyt://article/838a4413-6b50-5400-86e1-37730b81c84b'}, {'abstract': 'Swap out the garlic and try something new with lamb chops, served with a sauce of anchovies, capers and olives.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/05/dining/mediterranean-lamb-chops-recipe.html', 'snippet': 'Swap out the garlic and try something new with lamb chops, served with a sauce of anchovies, capers and olives.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the Mediterranean pantry, olive oil reigns. Black and green olives in varying hues and sizes abound. Capers, the pickled flower buds of a wild vine, are a constant, too, at the ready with their briny, floral scent. Most cooks in the region use anchovies, preserved in salt or oil, in their daily cooking, in whole fillets, chopped or pounded in a mortar. Wild thyme, rosemary and oregano are the herbs of choice, and garlic is a given.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/10/dining/10KITCHEN1/10KITCHEN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/10/dining/10KITCHEN1/10KITCHEN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'c