, 'byline': {'original': 'By Casey Schwartz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Casey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwartz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/65b930c5-a912-5710-a01e-105d52e8efd7', 'word_count': 1820, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65b930c5-a912-5710-a01e-105d52e8efd7'}, {'abstract': '“People in Punjab wear very bright, vibrant colors,” said Mark Hartman, a photographer based in Brooklyn who spent March in India. “To me, it shows a sense of pride and confidence.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/06/style/capturing-the-colorful-style-of-punjab-india.html', 'snippet': '“People in Punjab wear very bright, vibrant colors,” said Mark Hartman, a photographer based in Brooklyn who spent March in India. “To me, it shows a sense of pride and confidence.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'What keeps Mr. Hartman going back — this was his fifth trip — is his fascination with taking pictures of the Nihang Sikhs.\xa0This time, he spent most of his days posted outside the Golden Temple, the highest place of pilgrimage in Sikhism, as well as at the train station in Amritsar and the market in Chandigarh.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Capturing the Colorful Style of Punjab, India', 'kicker': 'the look', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Golden Temple (Amritsar, India)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sikhs and Sikhism', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Beards and Mustaches', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-06T10:01:04+0000', 'document_t