fe-5187-a231-ee9119e001c2', 'word_count': 1326, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0678111d-0cfe-5187-a231-ee9119e001c2'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Miéville, usually a writer of imaginative fiction, talks about his latest work, “October,” a nonfiction book about Russian history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/07/books/tell-us-5-things-about-your-book-china-mieville-on-the-russian-revolution.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Miéville, usually a writer of imaginative fiction, talks about his latest work, “October,” a nonfiction book about Russian history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'China Miéville is one of those fiction writers whose multivalent imagination — with its monsters, cityscapes of the future, and battles between good and evil — is capable of making readers’ heads explode. In The New York Times, Sarah Lyall once wrote that his novels “skitter among genres, magpie-ing elements from science fiction, fantasy, urban fantasy, traditional fairy tales, steampunk, horror.” So perhaps the weirdest thing Miéville could do at this point is write about the real world, which is what he does in “October,” his new nonfiction book about the Russian Revolution in 1917. Below, he tells us about his interest in the subject, why he chose to write about it a century after the events he describes, and more.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/08/arts/08BOOKMIEVILLE2/08BOOKMIEVILLE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 480, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/08/arts/08BOOKMIEVILLE2/08BOOKMIEVILLE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 480}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/08/arts/08BOOKMIEVILLE2/08BOOKMIEVILLE2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 819, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/08/arts/08BOOKMIEVILLE2/08BOOKMIEVILLE2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1639, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/08/arts/08BOOKMIEVILLE2/08BOOKMIEVILLE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/08/arts/08BOOKMIEVILLE2/08BOOKMIEVILLE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/08/arts/08BOOKMIEVILLE2/08BOOKMIEVILLE2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tell Us 5 Things About Your Book: China Miéville on the Russian Revolution', 'kicker': 'Q. & A.', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Russian Revolution, Without Fiction', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Russian Revolution (1917)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mieville, China', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-07T21:17:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/85b3a666-759a-5632-a147-6c0d443de9ee', 'word_count': 849, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85b3a666-759a-5632-a147-6c0d443de9ee'}, {'abstract': 'The student performers of Mannes Opera revived the 1958 Pulitzer Prize-winning work, which still hasn’t secured the spot in the repertory it deserves.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/07/arts/music/vanessa-review-mannes-opera.html', 'snippet': 'The student performers of Mannes Opera revived the 1958 Pulitzer Prize-winning work, which still hasn’t secured the spot in the repertory it deserves.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s not surprising that Mannes Opera’s new production of Samuel Barber’s “Vanessa” focuses on the dreamier elements of the 1958 work. This is almost inevitable when student singers (here those of the Mannes School of Music) portray its tortured characters. And there is certainly a nostalgic element in the piece, which tells the story of a rich, beautiful woman who has spent 20 reclusive years clinging to the hope that the lover who left her will return.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/08/arts/08MANNES/08MANNES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/08/arts/08MANNES/08MANNES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/08/arts/08MANNES/08MANNES-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/08/arts/08MANNES/08MANNES-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/08/arts/08MANNES/08MANNES-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'