one, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/07/fashion/07LOVE/07LOVE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/07/fashion/07LOVE/07LOVE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 382, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/07/fashion/07LOVE/07LOVE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 382}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/07/fashion/07LOVE/07LOVE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 651, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/07/fashion/07LOVE/07LOVE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1272, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Are You the Same Person on Social Media as You Are in Real Life?', 'kicker': 'Student Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reading and Writing Skills (Education)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-09T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Learning', 'section_name': 'The Learning Network', 'byline': {'original': 'By Caroline Crosson Gilpin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Caroline', 'middlename': 'Crosson', 'lastname': 'Gilpin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/84309605-c6cd-5b18-a631-dc8aa634a92a', 'word_count': 514, 'uri': 'nyt://article/84309605-c6cd-5b18-a631-dc8aa634a92a'}, {'abstract': 'Roy DeCarava wasn’t photographing giants of jazz. He was photographing the people and places he knew and understood.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/09/magazine/the-intimacy-behind-jazzs-seminal-image.html', 'snippet': 'Roy DeCarava wasn’t photographing giants of jazz. He was photographing the people and places he knew and understood.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s quite easy to tell the story of how certain photographs were taken, to link what’s going on within the frame to the situation or circumstances around it. In the case of Robert Capa’s gray, sea-drenched images of soldiers struggling to shore on D-Day, we know the precise historical moment they depict, what happened before and after. In other instances, photographs contain an implied narrative that can be expanded and expounded upon. Here all we have are two faces in tight close-up, so slurred and dimly lit that the picture barely succeeds in the most basic task of photographic portraiture: to enable us to identify whom we are looking at. Would you be able to see that this is John Coltrane and Ben Webster without being told? Given the uncertainty, it makes sense to begin by mentioning what the photograph is not.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/magazine/14onphoto1/14mag-14onphoto-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/14/magazine/14onphoto1/14mag-14onphoto-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/magazine/14onphoto1/14mag-14onphoto-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/magazine/14onphoto1/14mag-14onphoto-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 466, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/14/magazine/14onphoto1/14mag-14onphoto-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 466}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/magazine/14onphoto1/14mag-14onphoto-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 796, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/magazine/14onphoto1/14mag-14onphoto-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1592, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Intimacy Behind Jazz’s Seminal Image', 'kicker': 'On Photography', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Intimacy Behind Jazz’s Seminal Image', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coltrane, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Webster, Ben (1909-73)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'DeCarava, Roy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-09T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Geoff Dyer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Geoff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dyer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b577cb61-f483-5d05-93bd-9a15a3d28b76', 'word_count': 1606, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b577cb61-f483-5d05-93bd-9a15a3d28b76'}, {'abstract': 'It used to signal a quiet, anonymous life. Now privacy is a premium that may be out of reach for ordinary citizens.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/09/magazine/how-privacy-became-a-commodity-for-the-rich-and-powerful.html', 'snippet': 'It used to signal a quiet, anonymous life. Now privacy is a premium that may be out of reach for ordinary citizens.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Recently I handed over the keys to my email account to a service that promised to turn my spam-bloated inbox into a sparkling model of efficiency in just a few clicks. Unroll.me’s method of instant unsubscribing from newsletters and junk mail was “trusted by millions of happy users,” the site said, among them the “Scandal” actor Joshua Malina, who tweeted in 2014: “Your inbox will sing!” Plus, it was free. When a privacy policy popped up, I swatted away the legalese and tapped “continue.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/magazine/14firstwords/14firstwords-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/14/magazine/14firstwords/14firstwords-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/magazine/14firstwords/14firstwords-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/magazine/14firstwords/14firstwords-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/magazine/14firstwords/14firstwords-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/14/magazine/14firstwords/14firstwords-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/magazine/14firstwords/14firstwords-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Privacy Became a Commodity for the Rich and Powerful', 'kicker': 'First Words', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Open Secrets', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privacy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'English Language', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Unroll.me Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Uber Technologies Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-09T09:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amanda Hess', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amanda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hess', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8acd56fc-295a-50a0-8d2d-78300f5e5bd1', 'word_count': 1570, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8acd56fc-295a-50a0-8d2d-78300f5e5bd1'}, {'abstract': 'Are we kidding ourselves when we insist some things just aren’t for sale?', 'web_url': 'https://www