anization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5dd8f939-961d-58e6-87d0-255a82015cc3', 'word_count': 888, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5dd8f939-961d-58e6-87d0-255a82015cc3'}, {'abstract': 'One writer explores foreign murder mysteries as a way of seeing the world — and learning the gritty truth about other cultures.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/09/t-magazine/entertainment/best-international-crime-dramas-tv.html', 'snippet': 'One writer explores foreign murder mysteries as a way of seeing the world — and learning the gritty truth about other cultures.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Detective chief inspector Jane Tennison was the gateway drug. As played by the actress Helen Mirren on the British series “Prime Suspect,” she triggered my now-entrenched addiction to international crime dramas. Tennison — a relentlessly driven, hard-living, sexually indiscriminate female detective (as written by Lynda La Plante) — was exceptional and revolutionary; when the show debuted here in 1992, she had no equivalents on American TV. And Mirren was abetted by an equally riveting costar: the city of London as I’d never seen it — grubby and bristling with colorful miscreants.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '106', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/09/t-magazine/09tmag-crimetv1/09tmag-crimetv1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/09/t-magazine/09tmag-crimetv1/09tmag-crimetv1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/09/t-magazine/09tmag-crimetv1/09tmag-crimetv1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo