nd Executive Changes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-10T15:48:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth Paton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Paton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1acf4ebb-4643-5484-bdc1-8893a4079b2a', 'word_count': 301, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1acf4ebb-4643-5484-bdc1-8893a4079b2a'}, {'abstract': 'At the Grolier Club, the resurgence of modern calligraphy in the West will be represented by 70 masters of ink and pen.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/10/arts/design/the-calligraphy-revival-fine-handwriting-with-a-flourish.html', 'snippet': 'At the Grolier Club, the resurgence of modern calligraphy in the West will be represented by 70 masters of ink and pen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the rarefied environs of the Upper East Side, the bibliophilic Grolier Club qualifies as an alternative exhibition space. A small nonprofit, it is as devoted to the margins of culture as its downtown counterparts. But instead of focusing on overlooked artists, its mission is the neglected medium of books, foremost writing, illustrating, printing and collecting — but only foremost. The last Grolier show concerned engravings of United States securities. Its next, opening Wednesday, May 17, examines modern calligraphy in the West, which is more obscure than medieval or Asian. “The Calligraphy Revival: 1906 to 2016” traces its resurgence from the British scribe Edward Johnston (1872-1944) and the German type designer Rudolf Koch (1876-1934) through their students’ work and beyond. The 70 calligraphers include the polymath and type designer Hermann Zapf (1918-2015); he learned calligraphy from manuals by Johnston and Koch. Remember: “Calli” is Greek for beautiful. (Through July 29, grolierclub.org.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/arts/14GUIDE1/14GUIDE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/14/arts/14GUIDE1/14GUIDE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/arts/14GUIDE1/14GUIDE1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/arts/14GUIDE1/14GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 608, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/14/arts/14GUIDE1/14GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 608}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/arts/14GUIDE1/14GUIDE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1011, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/arts/14GUIDE1/14GUIDE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2022, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Calligraphy Revival’: Fine Handwriting, With a Flourish', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Art; Fine Handwriting, With a Flourish', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Grolier Club', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Calligraphy Revival: 1906 to 2016 (Exhibit)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-10T15:56:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roberta Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roberta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e7f910a1-7f3f-5acc-8981-07bf5f96e19e', 'word_count': 151, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e7f910a1-7f3f-5acc-8981-07bf5f96e19e'}, {'abstract': 'The ensemble will play works by Caroline Shaw and Hannah Lash at the New School, and Mozart at Lincoln Center’s Rose Studio.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/10/arts/music/calidore-string-quartet-new-school-lincoln-center.html', 'snippet': 'The ensemble will play works by Caroline Shaw and Hannah Lash at the New School, and Mozart at Lincoln Center’s Rose Studio.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a scene crowded with excellent young ensembles, the Calidore String Quartet can assert itself with pride, having recently won both a Borletti-Buitoni Trust Fellowship and the M-Prize International Chamber Music Competition. New Yorkers have two chances to judge for themselves this week. On Sunday, May 14, the quartet plays new works by Caroline Shaw and Hannah Lash in a concert at the New School that also features works for voice and mixed instruments by Luciano Berio and Matthew Ricketts. (newschool.edu.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/arts/14GUIDE2/14GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/14/arts/14GUIDE2/14GUIDE2-articleLa