, 'uri': 'nyt://article/094c6532-aeea-5020-95d3-c2c634b3d0ff'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Majok, who grew up in working-class New Jersey, has fleshed out her short work “John, Who’s Here From Cambridge” into a larger piece.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/10/theater/martyna-majoks-cost-of-living-scrambling-to-survive-together.html', 'snippet': 'Ms. Majok, who grew up in working-class New Jersey, has fleshed out her short work “John, Who’s Here From Cambridge” into a larger piece.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two summers ago, when Martyna Majok’s short play “John, Who’s Here From Cambridge” burst onto the stage of Ensemble Studio Theater, watching it was the kind of knock-you-sideways experience that made you grateful to be in the room. A quietly shattering meditation on the nature of privilege, it was funny, too — and exquisitely tender toward both of its characters, a wealthy graduate student with cerebral palsy and the young woman he hires to be his aide.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/arts/14GUIDE4/14GUIDE4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 988, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/14/arts/14GUIDE4/14GUIDE4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 988}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/arts/14GUIDE4/14GUIDE4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 623, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/arts/14GUIDE4/14GUIDE4-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1245, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/arts/14GUIDE4/14GUIDE4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/14/arts/14GUIDE4/14GUIDE4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/arts/14GUIDE4/14GUIDE4-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Martyna Majok’s ‘Cost of Living’: Scrambling to Survive, Together', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Theater; Want to Survive? Lean and Scramble', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Manhattan Theater Club', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Majok, Martyna', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Cost of Living (Play)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-10T16:01:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laura Collins-Hughes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laura', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Collins-Hughes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa02b386-6db8-5421-adfb-603f135942d0', 'word_count': 157, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa02b386-6db8-5421-adfb-603f135942d0'}, {'abstract': 'Perhaps you’ve forgotten what goes into an essential swashbuckler. Let “The Adventures of Robin Hood” (1938), with Errol Flynn, remind you.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/10/movies/a-robin-hood-of-swords-swagger-and-spectacle-at-film-forum.html', 'snippet': 'Perhaps you’ve forgotten what goes into an essential swashbuckler. Let “The Adventures of Robin Hood” (1938), with Errol Flynn, remind you.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What defines a swashbuckler? Swordplay, of course. Spectacle. A stirring score — say, by Erich Wolfgang Korngold — to make blood flow and spirits soar. And above all, a luminous star of physical prowess and palpable romantic presence. “The Adventures of Robin Hood” (1938), at Film Forum on Saturday, May 20, has all of the above, as well as a sterling leading lady (a sturdy, smart, radiant Olivia de Havilland) and a debonair villain (Basil Rathbone, a skilled swordsman in real life). But at its heart is a fierce, insolent and compassionate Errol Flynn, the dashing embodiment of the genre, in the role of a lifetime.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/arts/14GUIDE5/14GUIDE5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/14/arts/14GUIDE5/14GUIDE5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/arts/14GUIDE5/14GUIDE5-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/arts/14GUIDE5/14GUIDE5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 336, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/14/arts/14GUIDE5/14GUIDE5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 336}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/arts/14GUIDE5/14GUIDE5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 573, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subty