ast enough and Facebook is treating Snapchat like its own research and development lab.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/11/business/dealbook/snaps-disappearing-valuation.html', 'snippet': 'The social media company is burning cash, new users aren’t showing up fast enough and Facebook is treating Snapchat like its own research and development lab.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Snap investors may be feeling nauseated. The company, parent of the disappearing-message app Snapchat, reported a slowdown in user growth and a $2.2 billion quarterly loss. Meanwhile, rivals are co-opting features. These are worrisome trends, since its lofty $27 billion value hinges on attracting more people.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Snap’s Disappearing Valuation', 'kicker': 'Breakingviews', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Snap Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Company Reports', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-11T19:45:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'DealBook', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Saba', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Saba', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2207ba60-e25a-58b6-b542-cc15a59765a9', 'word_count': 345, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2207ba60-e25a-58b6-b542-cc15a59765a9'}, {'abstract': 'With works by Peter Saul and Raymond Pettibon, Antonio Sergio Bessa’s collection may not be for “the faint of heart” but it’s full of personal touches.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/11/arts/design/educating-through-visual-poetry.html', 'snippet': 'With works by Peter Saul and Raymond Pettibon, Antonio Sergio Bessa’s collection may not be for “the faint of heart” but it’s full of personal touches.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Antonio Sergio Bessa doesn’t consider himself a big-time collector. Yet there’s a blue-chip quality to the art on the walls of the apartment he shares with his husband, Ed Yanisch, in the Sugar Hill area of Harlem. There are museum-caliber names in the mix, like Raymond Pettibon, Kay Rosen, Ivan Serpa and Peter Saul. But it’s the stories behind the works that give this intimate collection its richness — and bite. “The collection, well, it’s an extension of me,” Mr. Bessa said.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/12/arts/12SHOWUSYOURWALL/12SHOWUSYOURWALL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/12/arts/12SHOWUSYOURWALL/12SHOWUSYOURWALL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/12/arts/12SHOWUSYOURWALL/12SHOWUSYOURWALL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/12/arts/12SHOWUSYOURWALL/12SHOWUSYOURWALL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/12/arts/12SHOWUSYOURWALL/12SHOWUSYOURWALL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/12/arts/12SHOWUSYOURWALL/12SHOWUSYOURWALL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/12/arts/12SHOWUSYOURWALL/12SHOWUSYOURWALL-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Educating Through Visual Poetry', 'kicker': 'Show Us Your Wall', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Educating Through Visual Poetry', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bessa, Antonio Sergio', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bronx Museum of the Arts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Collectors and Collections', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-11T19:46:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicole Herrington', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicole', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Herrington', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a61fac5-8329-5e78-a331-680feb4980a4', 'word_count': 690, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a61fac5-8329-5e78-a331-680feb4980a4'}, {'abstract': 'The editor of the magazine of the Writers Union of Canada quit after his call for an ‘Appropriation Prize’ caused an outcry.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/11/arts/editor-resigns-over-article-defending-cultural-appropriation.html', 'snippet': 'The editor of the magazine of the Writers Union of Canada quit after his call for an ‘Appropriation Prize’ caused an outcry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The simmering debate over cultural appropriation took another turn on Wednesday, when the Writers Union of Canada apologized for a magazine editorial defending the right of white authors to create characters from other backgrounds, and announced that its author had resigned.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editor Resigns Over an Article Defending ‘Cultural Appropriation’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Cultural Appropriation’ Leads to Resignation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Writers Union of Canada', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Niedzviecki, Hal', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Canada', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-11T19:47:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Schuessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schuessler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/204b11f0-f4c7-54ef-8bb4-7bf15aabf322', 'word_count': 440, 'uri': 'nyt://article/204b11f0-f4c7-54ef-8bb4-7bf15aabf322'}, {'abstract': 'A Netflix adaptation deepens a classic children’s tale about an orphan trying to make her way in a difficult world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/11/arts/television/review-anne-with-an-e-is-a-rewarding-return-to-green-gables.html', 'snippet': 'A Netflix adaptation deepens a classic children’s tale about an orphan trying to make her way in a difficult world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When parents voice that perennial complaint — “Why can’t they make stories for children like they used to?” — a tale many have in mind is “Anne of Green Gables,” Lucy Maud Montgomery’s cherished 1908 novel about an exceedingly imaginative orphan.