959e-9bd2b7dd2c15'}, {'abstract': 'Selected by Matthew Zapruder.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/12/magazine/73.html', 'snippet': 'Selected by Matthew Zapruder.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This poem is a loose, contemporary sonnet in the spirit of Shakespeare’s Sonnet 73. Kim Addonizio, a poet known for playing the blues harmonica at her poetry readings, seems to reject the hegemony of high literature, stating her preference for “The Glow-Worm,” a song with its own complex provenance. Adapted from the 1902 operetta “Lysistrata” and recorded by Johnny Mercer, the Mills Brothers and others, it features the unlikely, parodic couplet (added by Mercer) “Thou aeronautical boll weevil/Illuminate yon woods primeval.” Selected by Matthew Zapruder', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/magazine/14poem/14mag-14poem-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 218, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/14/magazine/14poem/14mag-14poem-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 218}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/magazine/14poem/14mag-14poem-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 372, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/magazine/14poem/14mag-14poem-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 743, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/magazine/14poem/14mag-14poem-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail'