article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/06f3612b-50e9-5e04-aab3-d7e4202846f9', 'word_count': 1072, 'uri': 'nyt://article/06f3612b-50e9-5e04-aab3-d7e4202846f9'}, {'abstract': 'Kids act up in new picture books about wearing a gorilla suit, refusing to change your pants, and the joy of not wearing clothes at all.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/12/books/review/bookshelf-clothes-get-kids-acting-out.html', 'snippet': 'Kids act up in new picture books about wearing a gorilla suit, refusing to change your pants, and the joy of not wearing clothes at all.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With certain children (they know who they are), what to wear is not a decision that includes parental input. Jameson is one of these; everyone’s given up trying to come between him and his green pants. But when he’s asked to be in a wedding, it’s wear black pants — or miss out. Kraegel (“King Arthur’s Very Great Grandson”) again spins a heartwarming tale attuned to the emotional highs and lows of childhood and the amazing elasticity of family love. His watercolor and charcoal art is as warm and witty as the story itself.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/books/review/14russo1/14russo1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/14/books/review/14russo1/14russo1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017