rgewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 650}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/13/business/13RUTERNBERG/13RUTERNBERG-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 946, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/13/business/13RUTERNBERG/13RUTERNBERG-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1892, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Trump’s White House Press Briefings, No Degree of Accuracy Required', 'kicker': 'Mediator', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'White House Buries The Bar for Accuracy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-12T17:33:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Rutenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rutenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/08d5e449-2ba0-5301-bc54-73c77dafd501', 'word_count': 1050, 'uri': 'nyt://article/08d5e449-2ba0-5301-bc54-73c77dafd501'}, {'abstract': 'It’s a big city, with plenty to do, see, hear and watch. Here’s a sampling of cultural highlights this weekend and over the week ahead.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/12/arts/things-to-do-in-nyc-glass-menagerie-mothers-day.html', 'snippet': 'It’s a big city, with plenty to do, see, hear and watch. Here’s a sampling of cultural highlights this weekend and over the week ahead.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most children probably think of a scientist’s tools as instruments like microscopes and telescopes. It wasn’t so long ago, however, that pencils, brushes, paint and paper were just as important. The Drawing Center is revisiting that era every Saturday in May at the zoo, where it’s recreated the Department of Tropical Research, a field station for the trailblazing naturalist William Beebe (1877-1962), who visited South America and the Caribbean, taking along artists who produced colorful records. Open from 10 a.m. to 3 p.m. and presented in conjunction with the Drawing Center’s exhibition “Exploratory Works: Drawings From the Department of Tropical Research Field Expeditions,” the\xa0zoo’s station welcomes young visitors to the adventure with art materials, activity cards and marine specimens. Every hour, from 11 a.m. through 2 p.m., Beebe and one of his artists, Else Bostelmann, will join them in the form of actors from the Wildlife Theater Troupe, who will conduct a 30-minute science and drawing program. It culminates with sketching some wonderful live models: the zoo’s sea lions. LAUREL GRAEBER', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/12/arts/12KIDSCOLUMN1/12KIDSCOLUMN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 387, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/12/arts/12KIDSCOLUMN1/12KIDSCOLUMN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 387}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/12/arts/12KIDSCOLUMN1/12KIDSCOLUMN1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 660, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/12/arts/12KIDSCOLUMN1/12KIDSCOLUMN1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1321, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/12/arts/12KIDSCOLUMN1/12KIDSCOLUMN1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/12/arts/12KIDSCOLUMN1/12KIDSCOLUMN1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/12/arts/12KIDSCOLUMN1/12KIDSCOLUMN1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '10 Things to Do in NYC Now', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Culture (Arts)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-12T17:45:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Louis Lucero II', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Louis', 'middlename': 'Lucero', 'lastname': 'II', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e053cace-b06c-57a0-a8eb-853539ad0750', 'word_count': 1272, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e053cace-b06c-57a0-a8eb-853539ad0750'}, {'abstract': 'The artist painted the homes of the rich and stylish, including Diana Vreeland. At 94, he’s getting a gallery retrospective.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/12/fashion/jeremiah-goodman-artist-retrospective.html', 'snippet': 'The artist painted the homes of the rich and stylish, including Diana Vreeland. At 94, he’s getting a gallery retrospective.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It seems almost quaint now, but there was a time not long ago when the wealthy and celebrated would commission paintings of their homes — a portrait of the mantelpiece to hang above the mantelpiece. For nearly 60 years, the master of this genteel art form was Jeremiah Goodman.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/fashion/14GOODMAN2-WEB/14GOODMAN2-WEB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 492, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/14/fashion/14GOODMAN2-WEB/14GOODMAN2-WEB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 492}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/fashion/14GOODMAN2-WEB/14GOODMAN2-WEB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 840, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/fashion/14GOODMAN2-WEB/14GOODMAN2-WEB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1679, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/fashion/14GOODMAN2-WEB/14GOODMAN2-WEB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/14/fashion/14GOODMAN2-WEB/14GOODMAN2-WEB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/fashion/14GOODMAN2-WEB/14GOODMAN2-WEB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jeremiah Goodman, the Rembrandt of 20th-Century Rooms', 'kicker': 'Noted', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Artist Who Peeked Into the Homes of Stars', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goodman, Jeremiah', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dessin Fournir Gallery (Los Angeles, Calif)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-12T17:45:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Kurutz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kurutz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7befb705-264a-5548-ad53-dc268561ed89', 'word_count': 614, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7befb705-264a-5548-ad53-dc268561ed89'}, {'abstract': 'Before his starring role in the playoff elimination of the Rockets, Simmons was an undrafted player whose small investment in himself paid off.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/12/sports/basketball/spurs-rockets-jonathon-simmons-nba-playoffs.html', 'snippet': 'Before his starring role in the playoff elimination of the Rockets, Simmons was an undrafted player whose small investment in himself paid off.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HOUSTON — Ross Hodge caught the latest installment of Jonathon Simmons’s improbable story on television Thursday night, watching as Simmons, a second-year swingman for the San Antonio Spurs, darted through the lane in the first quarter and dunked on the Houston Rockets. It was a sign of more destruction to come.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/13/sports/13CACCIOLAweb1/13CACCIOLA1-thumbStandard.jpg',