}, {'firstname': 'Laura', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Neill", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/64df36dc-baad-5f3a-a57e-bb051a555cbe', 'word_count': 38, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64df36dc-baad-5f3a-a57e-bb051a555cbe'}, {'abstract': 'Little dispensers of hand sanitizer are popping up everywhere, but what sort of world does the product envision?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/12/magazine/letter-of-complaint-purell.html', 'snippet': 'Little dispensers of hand sanitizer are popping up everywhere, but what sort of world does the product envision?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Common law has a doctrine called “clean hands.” If you want to settle a dispute, the idea holds, it’s a good idea to have them. Let’s say you and your neighbor have a disagreement about who should pay for the damage done to your roof by a limb that fell from his tree; it’s best not to retaliate by setting his house on fire before you sue him. If you are planning on taking your business partner to court to recover the funds he embezzled, best not to have siphoned off any yourself. It will make your lawyer that much more sheepish before the judge. It will affect the damages you are awarded. Clean hands are a sign that you deserve to be healed from the injury you’ve suffered.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/10/magazine/10mag-purell/10mag-purell-articleLarge.gif', 'height': 656, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/10/magazine/10mag-purell/10mag-purell-articleLarge.gif', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 656}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/10/magazine/10mag-purell/10mag-purell-jumbo.gif', 'height': 840, 'width'