', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/8ffacc78-9964-5b08-9217-6b7d080076ce', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/8ffacc78-9964-5b08-9217-6b7d080076ce'}, {'abstract': 'Rob Stephenson’s photographs capture the contrasting environments and moods of a Queens neighborhood.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/12/nyregion/album-far-rockaway-rob-stephenson.html', 'snippet': 'Rob Stephenson’s photographs capture the contrasting environments and moods of a Queens neighborhood.', 'lead_paragraph': '“There swept out of the sea a song” is a line from a poem by Carl Sandburg about Far Rockaway, Queens. In Rob Stephenson’s photo series of the same name, Rockaway is also sea-swept: a land of trees and tarps rearranged by the wind, buildings shrouded in fog, boats lying on their side by the side of the road.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/nyregion/14ALBUM3/14ALBUM3-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/14/nyregion/14ALBUM3/14ALBUM3-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/nyregion/14ALBUM3/14ALBUM3-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/nyregion/14ALBUM3/14ALBUM3-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 750, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/14/nyregion/14ALBUM3/14ALBUM3-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 750}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/nyregion/14ALBUM3/14ALBUM3-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 820, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/nyregion/14ALBUM3/14ALBUM3-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1639, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Far Rockaway: City, Sea and Wilderness', 'kicker': 'Album', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Seaworthy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Far Rockaway (Queens, NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stephenson, Rob (1974- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-12T19:17:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metropolitan', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Annie Correal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Annie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Correal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5653a569-d33a-523d-9715-f81569f9b154', 'word_count': 295, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5653a569-d33a-523d-9715-f81569f9b154'}, {'abstract': 'Hockey, long frozen out of the fiction best-seller list, gets its due in Fredrik Backman’s “Beartown.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/12/books/review/a-different-kind-of-sports-novel.html', 'snippet': 'Hockey, long frozen out of the fiction best-seller list, gets its due in Fredrik Backman’s “Beartown.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'PUCKISH: Sports novels can sell. Over the past decade or so, the hardcover fiction list has included books about baseball (Chad Harbach’s “The Art of Fielding”) and football (John Grisham’s “Playing for Pizza”) and basketball (E. Lynn Harris’s “Basketball Jones”) — even bicycle racing, which was central to Chris Cleave’s “Gold.” But hockey novels have been in short supply, as Keith Gessen noted in a 2006 essay for the Book Review: “What is needed now,” he argued, “for the sake of both hockey and literature, is more hockey literature.” Enter Fredrik Backman, whose novel “Beartown,” at No. 10 in its second week on the list, concerns a Swedish youth league team and its fraught place in a declining town. As popular Swedish exports go, Backman is up there with Abba and Stieg Larsson; his debut, “A Man Called Ove,” has sold millions of copies, and most of his subsequent books have also been best sellers. But in “Beartown” he’s not skating by (so to speak) on his name alone; he really has a feel for the sport, and for sports in general. “Hockey is just a silly little game,” he writes. “We burn and bleed and cry, fully aware that the most the sport can give us, in the very best scenario, is incomprehensibly meager and worthless: just a few isolated moments of transcendence. …But what the hell else is life made of?”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/books/review/0521-BKS-LIST/0521-BKS-LIST-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/21/books/review/0521-BKS-LIST/0521-BKS-LIST-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/books/review/0521-BKS-LIST/0521-BKS-LIST-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 't