}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ratings (Audience Measurement)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-15T20:17:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Koblin, Michael M. Grynbaum and Sapna Maheshwari', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Koblin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/28ddd4cc-268c-58c4-9ac1-68aeaec2f53e', 'word_count': 1003, 'uri': 'nyt://article/28ddd4cc-268c-58c4-9ac1-68aeaec2f53e'}, {'abstract': '“Silent Voices,” at the Brooklyn Academy of Music, used sung and spoken texts to examine race, gender, socioeconomic inequality and gentrification.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/15/arts/music/review-brooklyn-youth-chorus-silent-voices-bam.html', 'snippet': '“Silent Voices,” at the Brooklyn Academy of Music, used sung and spoken texts to examine race, gender, socioeconomic inequality and gentrification.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Viewed from the outside, a choir is a unit, a multitude acting as one. But over the past year, the Brooklyn Youth Chorus has gone out of its way to pry open the unified facade and examine internal differences. On Friday at the Brooklyn Academy of Music, it presented a resulting work, “Silent Voices,” an evening-long choral exploration of some of the big issues troubling the ensemble’s young members: race, gender, socioeconomic inequality and gentrification, among others.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017