'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Espionage and Intelligence Services', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-16T07:21:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David S. Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1a139a0-ff2a-5cf2-87ae-84d30f321e5c', 'word_count': 733, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1a139a0-ff2a-5cf2-87ae-84d30f321e5c'}, {'abstract': 'Residents protest a plan to raze their reviled tenements and move them far out of town.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/16/opinion/moscow-protest-housing-resettlement.html', 'snippet': 'Residents protest a plan to raze their reviled tenements and move them far out of town.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the face of it, anyone living in one of the ubiquitous five-story tenements ringing central Moscow should be happy to move out. Hurriedly thrown up to ease the chronic housing shortage after World War II and designed to last no more than 25 years, the shoddy, crooked, crumbling buildings with their tiny apartments are known as “khrushchevki,” a play on Nikita Khrushchev, the Soviet leader under whom most of them were built, and “trushchyoby,” Russian for slums. Yet when Moscow’s municipal government offered residents equivalent apartments in modern buildings farther from town so that thousands of the tenements could be razed, they promptly took to the streets.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/16/opinion/16tue3web/16tue3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 366, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/16/opinion/16tue3web/16tue3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 366}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/16/opinion/16tue3web/16tue3web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 625, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/16/opinion/16tue3web/16tue3web-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1250, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/16/opinion/16tue3web/16tue3web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/16/opinion/16tue3web/16tue3web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/16/opinion/16tue3web/16tue3web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Muscovites Fight for a Slum of Their Own', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Muscovites Fight for a Slum of Their Own', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Putin, Vladimir V', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Moscow (Russia)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-16T07:21:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/f4db5d6b-ab10-5ab3-a8b1-9d1792cc47e8', 'word_count': 464, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f4db5d6b-ab10-5ab3-a8b1-9d1792cc47e8'}, {'abstract': 'They need to help people on the wrong side of America’s new dividing line: the four-year college degree.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/16/opinion/democrats-working-class-votes.html', 'snippet': 'They need to help people on the wrong side of America’s new dividing line: the four-year college degree.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The great new dividing line in American life is the four-year college degree. The line runs through virtually every part of society.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/16/opinion/16leonhardtWeb/16leonhardtWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 382, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/16/opinion/16leonhardtWeb/16leonhardtWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 382}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/16/opinion/16leonhardtWeb/16leonhardtWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 653, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/16/opinion/16leonhardtWeb/16leonhardtWeb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1305, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/16/opinion/16leonhardtWeb/16leonhardtWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/16/opinion/16leonhardtWeb/16leonhardtWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/16/opinion/16leonhardtWeb/16leonhardtWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Democrats Can Get Their Mojo Back', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How the Left Can Get Its  Mojo Back', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Center for American Progress', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Minimum Wage', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-16T07:21:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Leonhardt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leonhardt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d852d99-5e8d-57c1-9361-03478f0de959', 'word_count': 804, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d852d99-5e8d-57c1-9361-03478f0de959'}, {'abstract': 'Susan Faludi’s latest book, about her photographer father’s transition to a woman, originally had no photos. In time she realized that images are moments carved disingenuously out of continuous experience, but they can be redeemed by age.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2017/05/16/susan-faludi-in-my-world-photographs-lie/', 'snippet': 'Susan Faludi’s latest book, about her photographer father’s transition to a woman, originally had no photos. In time she realized that images are moments carved disingenuously out of continuous experience, but they can be redeemed by age.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My father, Stefanie Faludi, died just before dawn on May 14, 2015, in the woman’s ward of a Budapest hospital, at the age of 87. It had been 11 years since her sex reassignment surgery and, not coincidentally, 11 years since our reunion after a quarter-century of estrangement. In the days after her death, I combed through her house, a forbidding concrete bunker perched high in the Buda Hills, inspecting the many closets, cabinets and storerooms she’d kept locked. I was looking for a clue to a life just passed, an inkling of who she was. Under the eaves behind two sets of deadbolts, and tucked inside steel cases, storage bins and acetate envelopes, I discovered an arsenal of cameras – Hasselblad, Rolleiflex, Leica, Olympus, a bellows camera built into a mahogany frame – lenses, cables, filters, tripods, along with developing sinks and processing trays, a wall-mounted enlarger and giant drum print-dryer, relics of her commercial photo business in Manhattan in the 1980s, her documentary work of the Brazilian interior in the 1950s, and her first attempts at filmmaking in Hungary in the 1930s. And, in acetate envelopes and slide trays, thousands of Tri-X negatives and Ektachrome slides, along with contact sheets and prints, chronicling year by year and in meticulous order, the minutiae of Faludi family life. That is: my life, and my life with my father. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Susan Faludi: ‘In My World, Photographs Lie’', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transgender and Transsexuals', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Avedon, Richard', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Faludi, Susan', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Auschwitz Concentration Camp', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Conde Nast Publications Inc', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Budapest (Hungary)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-16T08:00:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susan Faludi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Faludi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e4e90720-68d8-515e-a20c-83557660a20e', 'word_count': 3687, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e4e90720-68d8-515e-a20c-83557660a20e'}, {'abstract': 'Is Google exploiting public school teachers and students, or adding great value instead?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/16/learning/questions-for-how-google-took-over-the-classroom.html', 'snippet': 'Is Google exploiting public school teachers and students, or adding great value instead?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Article: How Google Took Over the Classroom', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/14/business/14EDGOOGLE-print/00EDGOOGLE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/14/business/14EDGOOGLE-print/00EDGOOGLE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnail