nk': 1}, {'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dennison', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Veda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shastri', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}, {'firstname': 'Joshua', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thomas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 4}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/2a165690-8286-55f0-9e37-1ea1eb186515', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/2a165690-8286-55f0-9e37-1ea1eb186515'}, {'abstract': 'Four years of free public college might be unrealistic, but two years could help students find better jobs, and keep the country’s economy competitive.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/16/magazine/should-students-get-grades-13-and-14-free-of-charge.html', 'snippet': 'Four years of free public college might be unrealistic, but two years could help students find better jobs, and keep the country’s economy competitive.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Free college is a lovely, sensible notion — but countries seem capable of embracing it only when enough of their top earners pay 50 percent or more of their income in taxes. In the United States, New York is the sole state to have taken the step of offering to cover tuition at all its public colleges and universities. And even this new benefit, the Excelsior Scholarship, which Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo signed into law last month, helps only a small fraction of the state’s residents — households earning less than $125,000 that are not already covered by financial aid — and their grants convert into loans unless graduates both live and work in the state for as many years as they benefited from the program. It also does not cover living costs.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21onmoney1/21onmoney1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 483, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21onmoney1/21onmoney1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 483}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21onmoney1/21onmoney1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 824, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21onmoney1/21onmoney1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1255, 'width': 1560, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21onmoney1/21onmoney1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21onmoney1/21onmoney1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21onmoney1/21onmoney1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Should Students Get ‘Grades 13 and 14’ Free of Charge?', 'kicker': 'On Money', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Should Students Get ‘Grades 13 and 14’ Free of Charge?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Financial Aid (Education)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'For-Profit Schools', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tuition', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pell Grants', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-16T09:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gary Rivlin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rivlin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/249114c5-cf42-5950-9934-88482afb509e', 'word_count': 1429, 'uri': 'nyt://article/249114c5-cf42-5950-9934-88482afb509e'}, {'abstract': 'A mutation in some obesity-prone dog breeds might reveal new risk factors for obesity in humans — and perhaps give rise to new drugs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/16/magazine/the-genetics-of-pooched-out-pooches.html', 'snippet': 'A mutation in some obesity-prone dog breeds might reveal new risk factors for obesity in humans — and perhaps give rise to new drugs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What lengths will a dog go to for a bite of sausage? Last November, scientists at the University of Cambridge in Britain persuaded several dozen pet owners to bring their Labrador retrievers to its veterinary school for a true test of will. Inside a mostly empty white room, a research associate let each dog sniff a hot dog before demonstratively placing it inside a small plastic hamster cage on the floor and sealing it shut with black duct tape. Some of the Labs showed only passing interest in the trapped sausage and spent more time exploring the rest of the room. But others stayed laser-focused on the treat. One in particular, a black Labrador named Ash, went into a tizzy, banging the cage around and not giving up until he pried the tape loose with his teeth and ate the hot dog.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '44', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21dog1/21dog1-thumbStandard-v5.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21dog1/21dog1-thumbStandard-v5.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21dog1/21dog1-thumbLarge-v5.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21dog1/21dog1-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 750, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21dog1/21dog1-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 750}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21dog1/21dog1-jumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 820, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21dog1/21dog1-superJumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 1932, 'width': 1546, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Genetics of Pooched-Out Pooches', 'kicker': 'The Health Issue', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pooched Out', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Obesity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Genetics and Heredity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dogs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-16T09:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roxanne Khamsi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roxanne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Khamsi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c26a6bc-da4b-54a3-95fa-b4fe825a4323', 'word_count': 2498, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c26a6bc-da4b-54a3-95fa-b4fe825a4323'}, {'abstract': 'We all may be losing sight of the difference between appearance and reality — between what we advertise and what we do.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/16/magazine/from-wells-fargo-to-fyre-festival-the-scam-economy-is-entering-its-baroque-phase.html', 'snippet': 'We all may be losing sight of the difference between appearance and reality — between what we advertise and what we do.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the past few decades, as the regulatory and legal protections that once shielded consumers, workers and investors have been systematically rolled back, the character of American business has noticeably changed. Year by year, we’ve reverted toward a 19th-century style of capitalism, one in which the caveat is almost always up to the emptor. We’ve moved from an industrial economy to a consumer economy to a service economy to an information economy to what you might call a flagrant-exploitation economy — one in which branding and “storytelling” have replaced advertising and possibly even reality. It’s not just that we’re being sold the sizzle more than the steak. It’s that we’re being sold the sizzle instead of and at the expense of the steak.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21firstwords/21mag-21firstwords-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 540, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21firstwords/21mag-21firstwords-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 540}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21firstwords/21mag-21firstwords-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 922, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21firstwords/21mag-21firstwords-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1396, 'width': 1550, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21firstwords/21mag-21firstwords-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21firstwords/21mag-21firstwords-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21firstwords/21mag-21firstwords-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'From Wells Fargo to Fyre Festival, the Scam Economy Is Entering Its Baroque Phase', 'kicker': 'First Words', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'False Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'English Language', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ja Rule', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McFarland, Billy (1991- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-16T09:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carina Chocano', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chocano', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/605b7cdd-b48c-50c3-98db-a2cfb23cfa9f', 'word_count': 1526, 'uri': 'nyt://article/605b7cdd-b48c-50c3-98db-a2cfb23cfa9f'}, {'abstract': 'Surrounding myself with animals to feel less alone was a mistake: The greatest comfort is in knowing their lives are not about us at all.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/16/magazine/what-animals-taught-me-about-being-human.html', 'snippet': 'Surrounding myself with animals to feel less alone was a mistake: The greatest comfort is in knowing their lives are not about us at all.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Long ago, when I was 9 or 10, I wrote a school essay on what I wanted to be when I grew up. I’ll be an artist, and I’ll keep a pet otter, I announced confidently, before adding, “as long as the otter is happy.” When I got back my exercise book, my teacher had commented, “But how can you tell if an otter is happy?” I boiled with indignation. Surely, I thought, otters would be happy if they had a soft place to sleep, could play, go exploring, make a friend (that would be me) and swim around in rivers catching fish. The fish were my only concession to the notion that an otter’s needs might not match my own. It never occurred to me that I might not understand the things an otter wanted, or understand much of what an otter might be. I thought animals were just like me.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '40', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21openeressay2/21openeressay2-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21openeressay2/21openeressay2-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21openeressay2/21openeressay2-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21openeressay2/21openeressay2-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 433, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21openeressay2/21openeressay2-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 433}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21openeressay2/21openeressay2-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 738, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21openeressay2/21openeressay2-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1476, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Animals Taught Me About Being Human', 'kicker': 'The Health Issue', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Bestiary of the Mind', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANIMALS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hawks (Birds)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-16T09:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Helen Macdonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Helen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macdonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e933c810-a8c8-5c9e-a7dc-3e0e24f9f6b2', 'word_count': 2044, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e933c810-a8c8-5c9e-a7dc-3e0e24f9f6b2'}, {'abstract': 'The language of bumbling politicians is elevated behind closed doors, where they’re free to express how much they despise one another.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/16/magazine/new-sentences-from-veep-season-6-episode-2.html', 'snippet': 'The language of bumbling politicians is elevated behind closed doors, where they’re free to express how much they despise one another.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One great joy of the Scottish director Armando Iannucci’s string of political satires — the BBC series “The Thick of It,” the film “In the Loop” and the HBO series “Veep,” now in its sixth season — is its characters’ relationship with language. Not one of them, including great statesmen and professional spokespeople, seems capable of making a well-formed, nonmortifying statement in public. But behind closed doors, where they’re free to express their endless contempt for one another, their speech is athletic and limber and just about symphonically profane. They’re masters of high-speed sophistry and self-serving rationalizations, and they threaten one another as if the power of threats lies mainly in how elegantly expressed they are — as when the former president in “Veep,” Selina Meyer, tells a congressman, “I will destroy you in ways that are so creative, they will honor me for it at the Kennedy Center.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21sentences/21mag-21sentences-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy':