waii', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-17T09:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nell McShane Wulfhart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nell', 'middlename': 'Mcshane', 'lastname': 'Wulfhart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ec7d379-3a15-54a7-b6ab-e9a8efd166ca', 'word_count': 604, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ec7d379-3a15-54a7-b6ab-e9a8efd166ca'}, {'abstract': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on ‘agent regret,’ the rapist next door and monitoring your child’s Internet activity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/17/magazine/i-accidentally-killed-a-child-may-i-get-in-touch-with-the-family.html', 'snippet': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on ‘agent regret,’ the rapist next door and monitoring your child’s Internet activity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thirty years ago, I was in a car-and-moped accident. I was driving the car. The police determined that the moped driver, a preteen with no driving experience, was at fault. A passenger on the moped, also a preteen, died the next day from her injuries. At the funeral, the parents made clear that they would not accept my apology and condolences, and that they never wanted to hear from me again.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21ethicist/21ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1055, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21ethicist/21ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1055}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21ethicist/21ethicist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 583, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21ethicist/21ethicist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1129, 'width': 642, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21ethicist/21ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21ethicist/21ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21ethicist/21ethicist-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'I Accidentally Killed a Child. May I Get in Touch With the Family?', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'I Accidentally Killed a Child. May I Contact The Family?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Traffic Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-17T09:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kwame Anthony Appiah', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kwame', 'middlename': 'Anthony', 'lastname': 'Appiah', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c927492e-32bb-5d03-ba39-6cc903973ca0', 'word_count': 1114, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c927492e-32bb-5d03-ba39-6cc903973ca0'}, {'abstract': 'The new entry in Ridley Scott’s long-lived franchise is committed to the canny management of expectations, but that doesn’t diminish its creepiness.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/17/movies/alien-covenant-review-ridley-scott.html', 'snippet': 'The new entry in Ridley Scott’s long-lived franchise is committed to the canny management of expectations, but that doesn’t diminish its creepiness.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the larval alien at last explodes out of a human torso, you may experience, along with the expected jolt of fright, a curious sensation of relief, even affection. So much has changed in the world, and in movies, since the first “Alien” freaked out audiences in 1979, that the appearance of a chittering, scurrying, fast-spawning extraterrestrial predator feels like a visit from an old friend. Humanity may have lapsed into terminal tedium or hubristic stupidity, but that creature, with long, slender limbs and a cranium like a speedskater’s helmet, remains interesting.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/19/arts/19ALIEN1/19ALIEN1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/19/arts/19ALIEN1/19ALIEN1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/19/arts/19ALIEN1/19ALIEN1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/19/arts/19ALIEN1/19ALIEN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/19/arts/19ALIEN1/19ALIEN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/19/arts/19ALIEN1/19ALIEN1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/19/arts/19ALIEN1/19ALIEN1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: ‘Alien: Covenant’ Stays on Brand With Its Terror', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Summoning Brand-Name Terror', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Alien: Covenant (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scott, Ridley', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-17T09:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a0f47c84-1c12-5a04-b9e2-14a4d2ad4c06', 'word_count': 847, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a0f47c84-1c12-5a04-b9e2-14a4d2ad4c06'}, {'abstract': 'The radio host on declaring his black pride on TV, never feeling embarrassed and the end of “Girls.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/17/magazine/charlamagne-tha-god-loves-telling-middle-america-about-black-privilege.html', 'snippet': 'The radio host on declaring his black pride on TV, never feeling embarrassed and the end of “Girls.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'As co-host of the radio program “The Breakfast Club,” you call yourself “the prime minister of pissing people off.” But your new book, “Black Privilege,” is less about aggravation and more about sharing principles that you think are integral to success. I’ve always been the person who, even in the ’hood, people would come to with their problems. At one point in my life, I thought I was going to be a psychiatrist.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '82', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21talk/21mag-21talk-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1175, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21talk/21mag-21talk-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1175}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21talk/21mag-21talk-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 523, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21talk/21mag-21talk-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1046, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21talk/21talk-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21talk/21talk-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21talk/21talk-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Charlamagne Tha God 