'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Sanders, M.d', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sanders', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fb13c49a-b4ed-59ea-b03f-a0535dcb7c2d', 'word_count': 1407, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fb13c49a-b4ed-59ea-b03f-a0535dcb7c2d'}, {'abstract': 'The cruelest farmyard animal is revealing of human nature.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/17/magazine/the-rooster-attacked-me-and-yet-i-loved-him.html', 'snippet': 'The cruelest farmyard animal is revealing of human nature.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I was growing up on a small farm in western New York State, my greatest thrill was gathering eggs from the nests in the coop and feeding our little flock of 40-odd chickens under the tutelage of my Hungarian grandmother. I most looked forward to seeing the barnyard roosters, whose belligerent, strutting, preening, aggressive macho behavior was a source of endless fascination and trepidation.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '63', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21sidebar-rooster/21sidebar-rooster-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21sidebar-rooster/21sidebar-rooster-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21sidebar-rooster/21sidebar-rooster-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21sidebar-rooster/21sidebar-rooster-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 646, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21sidebar-rooster/21sidebar-rooster-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 646}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21sidebar-rooster/21sidebar-rooster-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 952, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21sidebar-rooster/21sidebar-rooster-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1450, 'width': 1347, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Rooster Attacked Me — and Yet I Loved Him', 'kicker': 'The Health Issue', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Rooster', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chickens', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-17T09:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joyce Carol Oates', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joyce', 'middlename': 'Carol', 'lastname': 'Oates', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/51706a3f-85e4-533f-bb8a-e00eed39a6c3', 'word_count': 482, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51706a3f-85e4-533f-bb8a-e00eed39a6c3'}, {'abstract': 'Human families aren’t the only kind displaced by Lahore’s construction boom.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/17/magazine/the-kites-are-leaving.html', 'snippet': 'Human families aren’t the only kind displaced by Lahore’s construction boom.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My children live in the Lahore compound where I spent much of my own childhood, the fourth generation of my family to do so, with members of three of these generations presently alive and resident, including my parents, who built a house on part of the front lawn three decades ago, and my wife and me, who live in the old house, which was constructed three decades earlier. When I was a child, Lahore was home to three million people, and our neighborhood was a leafy, grassy expanse speckled with bungalows. Now Lahore is home to three times as many people, and our nearest neighbors are shopping malls, restaurants, apartment buildings, offices — crammed close together, with little green.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '60', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21sidebar-kites/21sidebar-kites-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 309, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21sidebar-kites/21sidebar-kites-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 309}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21sidebar-kites/21sidebar-kites-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 527, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21sidebar-kites/21sidebar-kites-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1054, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21sidebar-kites/21sidebar-kites-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21sidebar-kites/21sidebar-kites-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21sidebar-kites/21sidebar-kites-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Kites Are Leaving', 'kicker': 'The Health Issue', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Kites', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kites', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Lahore (Pakistan)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-17T09:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mohsin Hamid', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mohsin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hamid', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1a6a4c4-65e0-5f6d-9880-eb01a2e2ea54', 'word_count': 480, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1a6a4c4-65e0-5f6d-9880-eb01a2e2ea54'}, {'abstract': 'What I learned from the little shell.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/17/magazine/the-death-of-my-hermit-crab.html', 'snippet': 'What I learned from the little shell.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With a kid’s hubris, I was certain I could take care of two hermit crabs into their spiny dotage. It was fascinating to watch a naked crab emerge from the fluted lip of a shell, looking for a new home. I filled a tank with large and midrange conchs. Construction sites opened up all over the tank. Conchs scraped around the sand, levitating on burnt orange claws. The hermits buried and exhumed themselves at unpredictable intervals. They outgrew and shed their bodies and each time this happened, it was magic. It made bodies seem fungible, an armature you could shuck and swap. They moved like a glacial version of the con man’s shell game. They were master vanishers, digging down into the sandy substratum. Mine sometimes disappeared for days. Or they relocated at night, and I would spend a morning beaming affection at an empty shell. This was a little embarrassing. But it was also marvelous, to be so fooled.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '61', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21sidebar-hermitcrab/21sidebar-hermitcrab-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21sidebar-hermitcrab/21sidebar-hermitcrab-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21sidebar-hermitcrab/21sidebar-hermitcrab-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21sidebar-hermitcrab/21sidebar-hermitcrab-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 511, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21sidebar-hermitcrab/21sidebar-hermitcrab-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 511}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21sidebar-hermitcrab/21sidebar-hermitcrab-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 872, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21sidebar-hermitcrab/21sidebar-hermitcrab-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1229, 'width': 1444, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Death of My Hermit Crab', 'kicker': 'The Health Issue', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Hermit Crabs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crabs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Death and Dying', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-17T09:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Russell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Russell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/58d2e2f5-8e55-559c-bdbf-d60c61abcaca', 'word_count': 456, 'uri': 'nyt://article/58d2e2f5-8e55-559c-bdbf-d60c61abcaca'}, {'abstract': 'That audacious animal was a tether to home.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/17/magazine/the-mongoose-and-the-emigre.html', 'snippet': 'That audacious animal was a tether to home.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There were mongooses everywhere on the island, but in those years I only ever saw them in Azua, where my mother was from. When we visited that part of the country, which seemed to me as withered as Santo Domingo was lush, they never failed to appear — low-slung sweeps of light, streaking across a baseball field, vanishing into grass. Mongooses were irresistible to a kid like me. Even in that small place, their reputations preceded them: legendary ratters, breakers of serpents, lightning fast and smart like all get out. Naturally I wanted one for a pet, but my abuelo, who knew everything about every animal in Azua, just laughed. Jurones can’t be tamed, he explained. Son guapo.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '62', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21sidebar-mongoose/21mag-21sidebars-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21sidebar-mongoose/21mag-21sidebars-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21sidebar-mongoose/21mag-21sidebars-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 511, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21sidebar-mongoose/21mag-21sidebars-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 937, 'width': 1876, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21sidebar-mongoose/21mag-21sidebars-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21sidebar-mongoose/21mag-21sidebars-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21sidebar-mongoose/21mag-21sidebars-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Mongoose and the Émigré', 'kicker': 'The Health Issue', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Mongooses', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Santo Domingo (Dominican Republic)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-17T09:00:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Junot Díaz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Junot', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Díaz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/523621fd-3555-513e-b511-56442a50fd9b', 'word_count': 428, 'uri': 'nyt://article/523621fd-3555-513e-b511-56442a50fd9b'}, {'abstract': 'It’s humbling to care for an animal that reminds you, each day, of your own imminent death.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/17/magazine/a-pet-tortoise-who-will-outlive-us-all.html', 'snippet': 'It’s humbling to care for an animal that reminds you, each day, of your own imminent death.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every morning, Fred takes a walk around my parents’ yard in suburban Honolulu. The yard, though small, around 600 square feet, is beautiful, green and cool and jungly, densely planted with lacy native ferns and heavy-headed crimson heliconia and fragrant with white flowers: gardenia, plumeria, ginger, night-blooming jasmine. Fred is 15 years old and 80 pounds, and since my parents adopted him two years ago, he has never left this yard. When he is dozing in the shade, the old shower trees outside the picket fence that surrounds the yard rain their pink and yellow petals down on him.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '59', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21tortoise1/21mag-21tortoise-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21tortoise1/21mag-21tortoise-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 800}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21tortoise1/21mag-21tortoise-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21tortoise1/21mag-21tortoise-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1536, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21tortoise1/21mag-21tortoise-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21tortoise1/21mag-21tortoise-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21tortoise1/21mag-21tortoise-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Pet Tortoise Who Will Outlive Us All', 'kicker': 'The Health Issue', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fred Everlasting', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Turtles and Tortoises', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Love (Emotion)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hawaii', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pets', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-17T09:00:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hanya Yanagihara', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hanya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yanagihara', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e80ebb34-4850-5fa3-9303-2d2e5ba86530', 'word_count': 2896, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e80ebb34-4850-5fa3-9303-2d2e5ba86530'}, {'abstract': 'Why Burmese pythons may be the best way to study diabetes, heart disease and the protective effects of gastric-bypass surgery in humans.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/17/magazine/when-the-lab-rat-is-a-snake.html', 'snippet': 'Why Burmese pythons may be the best way to study diabetes, heart disease and the protective effects of gastric-bypass surgery in humans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As Amit Choudhary opened the package of snake blood, the first thing he noticed was its color. It looked like yogurt. The blood plasma, harvested from a Burmese python shortly after feeding, was so clogged with fatty acids that it was not clear but milky white. An oily mess like that should be toxic, Choudhary thought. Indeed, when he smeared the same amount of fatty acids on a plate of human pancreatic cells, the kind that supply the body with insulin, they self-destructed from the stress. Yet he knew the snake could somehow thrive, even as its plasma turned to yogurt after every single meal.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '65', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21snake1/21mag-21snake-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21snake1/21mag-21snake-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/magazine/21snake1/21mag-21snake-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType':