ect', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Reilly, Bill", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-18T15:14:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kelly Couturier and Prashant S. Rao', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kelly', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Couturier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Prashant', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Rao', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e67eecb1-e11a-588f-a52e-17c4ff2d9bb1', 'word_count': 813, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e67eecb1-e11a-588f-a52e-17c4ff2d9bb1'}, {'abstract': 'The writer and cookbook author Julia Turshen looks back on decades of delectable imagery in the new book "Feast for the Eyes."', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/18/t-magazine/food-photography-history.html', 'snippet': 'The writer and cookbook author Julia Turshen looks back on decades of delectable imagery in the new book "Feast for the Eyes."', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve always wanted a book like “Feast for the Eyes,” which charts the chronological history of the ways in which food has been photographed, recorded and shared for over a century. It starts in the 1800s, with the first photographs to depict food front and center, and brings us to the present, with the many kinds of images we are so used to seeing now. And with the evolution of food photography, we also see the evolution of style, technology, politics, media and even home cooking. We are reminded over and over that everything new is hardly ever new. \xa0', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url':