ifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e1263418-25aa-599d-b8cb-fdc107cbba16', 'word_count': 769, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e1263418-25aa-599d-b8cb-fdc107cbba16'}, {'abstract': 'The Pulitzer Prize-winning playwright Robert Schenkkan focuses on a near future with New York under martial law.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/18/theater/building-the-wall-imagines-life-after-the-terror-of-2019.html', 'snippet': 'The Pulitzer Prize-winning playwright Robert Schenkkan focuses on a near future with New York under martial law.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2019, two square blocks in Times Square have been “irradiated,” and in response, President Trump has imposed martial law. That is the backdrop of “Building the Wall,” a new two-character piece by the Pulitzer Prize-winning playwright Robert Schenkkan (“The Kentucky Cycle”) that is one of the first political dramas to imagine explicitly the darkest ramifications of the 2016 election.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/arts/21GUIDE1/21GUIDE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/21/arts/21GUIDE1/21GUIDE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/arts/21GUIDE1/21GUIDE1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/arts/21GUIDE1/21GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'le