': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-18T19:32:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SundayBusiness', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'DealBook', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Protess', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Protess', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/37f24f04-6ec8-5439-8a81-ec3779a7047f', 'word_count': 3517, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37f24f04-6ec8-5439-8a81-ec3779a7047f'}, {'abstract': 'The framers cared more about a president’s “maladministration” and potential future abuses than they did about his past crimes and misdemeanors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/18/opinion/impeachment-trump-constitution.html', 'snippet': 'The framers cared more about a president’s “maladministration” and potential future abuses than they did about his past crimes and misdemeanors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The simmering talk of impeachment swirling around President Trump largely concerns whether he committed a crime by asking James Comey, when he was still the director of the F.B.I., to end an investigation of Michael T. Flynn, the onetime national security adviser. From the perspective of criminal law, the resulting questions, which pertain mainly to the president’s intent in making the request, are inescapable. From the perspective of the decidedly political act of impeachment, they are irrelevant. The purpose of impeachment is not punitive. It is prophylactic.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/18/opinion/18weinerWeb/18weinerWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/18/opinion/18weinerWeb/18weinerWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbna