e': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Apuzzo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mazzetti', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3759c26c-03a9-50f2-a918-a969bd858007', 'word_count': 1033, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3759c26c-03a9-50f2-a918-a969bd858007'}, {'abstract': 'Pynchon’s “Mason & Dixon” is a book for these times. And, if you can put up with cassettes, an audiobook does exist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/19/books/review/thomas-pynchons-mason-dixon-essay-alexander-nazaryan.html', 'snippet': 'Pynchon’s “Mason & Dixon” is a book for these times. And, if you can put up with cassettes, an audiobook does exist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m not in the business of giving advice, but the following wisdom I dispense confidently: Do not go running while trying to listen to Thomas Pynchon’s “Mason & Dixon” on a cassette player. It’s frustration guaranteed, especially after the belt clip on your Walkman breaks off and you’re forced to carry the ungainly device in your hand. You have the next tape in the 20-cassette set rattling in a shorts pocket; in your other pocket is an iPhone, because obviously your Sony Walkman WM-FX303 is not connected to the internet, and you should probably be connected to the internet, lest during your bout of fitness the United States launches a war against Iceland or you’re commanded to stop at Whole Foods for milk.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/books/review/0521-BKS-Nazaryan/0521-BKS-Nazaryan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/21/books/review/0521-BKS-Nazaryan/0521-BKS-Nazaryan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/books/review/0521-BKS-Nazaryan/0521-BKS-Nazaryan-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/books/review/0521-BKS-Nazaryan/0521-BKS-Nazaryan-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 747, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/21/books/review/0521-BKS-Nazaryan/0521-BKS-Nazaryan-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 747}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/books/review/0521-BKS-Nazaryan/0521-BKS-Nazaryan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 823, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/21/books/review/0521-BKS-Nazaryan/0521-BKS-Nazaryan-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1145, 'width': 920, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Personal Foray Into the Long-Lost Pynchon Tapes', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Mason &amp; Dixon’ and Me', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pynchon, Thomas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Mason & Dixon (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-19T09:00:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexander Nazaryan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexander', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nazaryan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d4ce6750-f531-5c22-967e-8259c58b7bbf', 'word_count': 1415, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d4ce6750-f531-5c22-967e-8259c58b7bbf'}, {'abstract': 'Cashing out a retirement plan when switching jobs is generally bad — and risky. Better to roll it over yourself into an individual retirement account.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/19/your-money/401ks-and-similar-plans/do-not-drain-your-401-k-or-let-a-former-employer-do-it.html', 'snippet': 'Cashing out a retirement plan when switching jobs is generally bad — and risky. Better to roll it over yourself into an individual retirement account.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you are lucky enough to work for a paternalistic employer, it can be easier than ever to set yourself up with a retirement savings account. Many employers have taken to signing people up automatically for 401(k)’s and similar workplace plans. Some even raise the amount you set aside each year unless you object.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/20/business/20moneywiderillo/20moneywiderillo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 303, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/20/business/20moneywiderillo/20moneywiderillo-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 303}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/20/business/20moneywiderillo/20moneywiderillo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 517, 'width': 1024, 's