tion': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/822e05c8-1eda-5576-862f-b08a012b2583', 'word_count': 522, 'uri': 'nyt://article/822e05c8-1eda-5576-862f-b08a012b2583'}, {'abstract': 'Condoleezza Rice’s “Democracy” offers sober, high-minded rhetoric about America’s role in the world. It also notes Qaddafi’s schoolboy crush on her.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/19/books/review/from-condoleezza-rice-tales-of-diplomacy-and-unrequited-love.html', 'snippet': 'Condoleezza Rice’s “Democracy” offers sober, high-minded rhetoric about America’s role in the world. It also notes Qaddafi’s schoolboy crush on her.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MUSE OF MUAMMAR: Condoleezza Rice’s “Democracy” is new on the hardcover nonfiction list, at No. 4, and plenty of observers have already commented on its sober, high-minded rhetoric about America’s role in the world. So I’ll dish instead. Who knew that during Rice’s days as secretary of state, the former Libyan strongman Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi was besotted with her? “I support my darling black African woman,” he said of Rice during a 2007 interview with the network Al Jazeera. “I admire and am very proud of the way she leans back and gives orders to the Arab leaders.” He added: “Yes, Leezza, Leezza, Leezza. … I love her very much.” So when Rice included Tripoli on a visit to the Middle East the following year, there were some decidedly uncomfortable undercurrents in her meeting with Qaddafi. “It had been a really strange trip,” she writes, setting aside her usual diplomacy. “I awoke with that sensation one has after a bizarre dream. Did I really just sit in his kitchen and have dinner with his female security guards who many believe are also his daughters? Did he really give me a video — with pictures of me set to music — called ‘Black Flower in the White House’? O.K. Whatever his weird affection for you, thank the Lord, that visit is over. Time to move on.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/28/books/review/0528-BKS-LIST/0528-BKS-LIST-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/28/books/review/0528-BKS-LIST/0528-BKS-LIST-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/28/books/review/0528-BKS-LIST/0528-BKS-LIST-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/28/books/review/0528-BKS-LIST/0528-BKS-LIST-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 580, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/28/books/review/0528-BKS-LIST/0528-BKS-LIST-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 580}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/28/books/review/0528-BKS-LIST/0528-BKS-LIST-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 828, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/28/books/review/0528-BKS-LIST/0528-BKS-LIST-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 828, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'From Condoleezza Rice, Tales of Diplomacy and Unrequited Love', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rice, Condoleezza', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}]