's Sister (Book)", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Underdown, Beth', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-22T09:00:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Helen Castor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Helen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Castor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8588abfe-f4f6-5644-8a41-668241adcce8', 'word_count': 619, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8588abfe-f4f6-5644-8a41-668241adcce8'}, {'abstract': 'On the anniversary of the Six Day War, writers assess the perpetual limbo that followed, through the prisms of language, history and politics.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/22/books/review/new-books-about-six-day-war.html', 'snippet': 'On the anniversary of the Six Day War, writers assess the perpetual limbo that followed, through the prisms of language, history and politics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What new insight can a bunch of Very Important Writers provide about the Israeli occupation of the West Bank? Phrased a little differently — Michael Chabon and Ayelet Waldman call these novelists and essayists “storytellers” — this is the question the two editors of KINGDOM OF OLIVES AND ASH: Writers Confront the Occupation (Harper Perennial, paper, $16.99) decided to pose on the occupation’s 50th anniversary. (Moriel Rothman-Zecher was associate editor.) They gathered their friends, most of whom, they admit, had never given the occupation “more than a glancing consideration,” and took them on weeklong tours of East Jerusalem, the village of Susiya in the south Hebron hills and the cities of Hebron and Ramallah, all flash points where Israeli control over Palestinian lives can be felt acutely. The result is an exhausting collection of essays. And with a few strong exceptions — like the pieces by Dave Eggers, Rachel Kushner and Waldman herself — they are what you might expect: fairly superficial, full of unearned authority and exhibitionist empathy. A parachute job.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/28/books/review/28Beckerman1/28Beckerman1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 403, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/28/books/review/28Beckerman1/28Beckerman1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 403}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/28/books/review/28Beckerman1/28Beckerman1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 687, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/28/books/review/28Beckerman1/28Beckerman1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1113, 'width': 1658, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/28/books/review/28Beckerman1/28Beckerman1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/28/books/review/28Beckerman1/28Beckerman1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/28/books/review/28Beckerman1/28Beckerman1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '50 Years On, Stories of the Six Day War and What Came After', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'On the Se