rial': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe8a69b2-5ef3-5dcc-bebe-2f2f3a58de60', 'word_count': 301, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe8a69b2-5ef3-5dcc-bebe-2f2f3a58de60'}, {'abstract': 'Ellen Brown’s “Bloody Mary Book” details the history of this classic cocktail and includes inventive recipes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/22/dining/bloody-mary-book-brunch-drink.html', 'snippet': 'Ellen Brown’s “Bloody Mary Book” details the history of this classic cocktail and includes inventive recipes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nothing is easier than a Bloody Mary: Open a can of tomato-based mix, add vodka and garnish with a celery stick. It is about the only drink for which I would use a premixed base, and in a pinch I have been known to start with diluted ketchup. In this new book, you’ll find a guide to assembling the classic Bloody Mary and the freedom to experiment with variations like a Korean version seasoned with kimchi purée, a garden-fresh take with muddled cherry tomatoes, or a tomato water “martini.” The drink’s history and bar snacks are included. The broiled Parmesan-shallot toasts deserve to be added to the repertoire: “The Bloody Mary Book” by Ellen Brown (Running Press, $18).', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/24/dining/24BURNER-BOOK/24BURNER-BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/24/dining/24BURNER-BOOK/24BURNER-BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/24/dining/24BURNER-BOOK/24BURNER-BOOK-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': N