ity and Bisexuality', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dating and Relationships', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Teach for America', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mississippi', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-23T07:21:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dylan Lewis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dylan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lewis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/9eaff0f4-b14f-5218-b450-481eac475c04', 'word_count': 1216, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9eaff0f4-b14f-5218-b450-481eac475c04'}, {'abstract': 'Contemplating life after Trump.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/23/opinion/alienated-mind-trump-supporters.html', 'snippet': 'Contemplating life after Trump.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The campaign of 2016 was an education in the deep problems facing the country. Angry voters made a few things abundantly clear: that modern democratic capitalism is not working for them; that basic institutions like the family and communities are falling apart; that we have a college educated elite that has found ingenious ways to make everybody else feel invisible, that has managed to transfer wealth upward to itself, that crashes the hammer of political correctness down on anybody who does not have faculty lounge views.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/23/opinion/23brooksWeb/23brooksWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/23/opinion/23b