, {'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Iaboni', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'briefing', '_id': 'nyt://article/e68744c1-3f95-59f8-9d11-06d2ebad5a2f', 'word_count': 847, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e68744c1-3f95-59f8-9d11-06d2ebad5a2f'}, {'abstract': 'Jason Bourque’s thriller examines the moral compromises of meting out death from afar, and the unraveling of a home when a victim comes to supper.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/24/movies/drone-review.html', 'snippet': 'Jason Bourque’s thriller examines the moral compromises of meting out death from afar, and the unraveling of a home when a victim comes to supper.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jason Bourque’s modest, proficient thriller “Drone” scrutinizes the ethics of warfare by remote control, an issue dramatized with greater effectiveness in Gavin Hood’s “Eye in the Sky,” from 2016, and Andrew Niccol’s “Good Kill,” from 2015. Those films examined the moral struggles of military personnel as they conduct missions with clinical precision while far removed from danger. “Drone” sticks to the home front, following Neil Wistin (Sean Bean), a drone pilot on assignments for a covert C.I.A. contractor in Washington State, and Imir Shaw (Patrick Sabongui), a visitor to his home with an ulterior motive.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/26/arts/26DRONE/26DRONE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/26/arts/26DRONE/26DRONE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/26/arts/26DRONE/26DRONE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subT