], 'headline': {'main': 'Ben Carson Calls Poverty a ‘State of Mind,’ Igniting a Backlash', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Comments On Poverty Start Uproar', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poverty', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carson, Benjamin S', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Housing and Urban Development Department', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Affordable Housing', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Area Planning and Renewal', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-25T07:14:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Yamiche Alcindor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Yamiche', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alcindor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9a52656-ab58-5481-ab8f-28aa4eeb227d', 'word_count': 336, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9a52656-ab58-5481-ab8f-28aa4eeb227d'}, {'abstract': 'Conflict rules are skirted in secret, and inquiries from those responsible for oversight are rebuffed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/25/opinion/trump-white-house-ethics-office.html', 'snippet': 'Conflict rules are skirted in secret, and inquiries from those responsible for oversight are rebuffed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It takes a serious commitment to incompetence and deception to spawn as many ethical and legal concerns as the Trump administration has in just four months. The misbehavior by White House officials in the past few days has been impressive even by Trumpian standards. They’ve tried to raise doubts about the independence of the special counsel investigating the Trump campaign’s ties to Russia. And they’ve stonewalled efforts by the Office of Government Ethics to identify conflicts of interest in the administration.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/25/opinion/25thu1/25thu1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 343, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/25/opinion/25thu1/25thu1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 343}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/25/opinion/25thu1/25thu1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 585, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/25/opinion/25thu1/25thu1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1170, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/25/opinion/25thu1/25thu1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/25/opinion/25thu1/25thu1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/25/opinion/25thu1/25thu1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The White House’s Aversion to Ethical Scrutiny', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Aversion to Ethical Scrutiny', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics and Official Misconduct', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conflicts of Interest', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Office of Government Ethics', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mueller, Robert S III', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Special Prosecutors (Independent Counsel)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-25T07:21:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa96259d-8000-5f8d-9e5c-a8c78c2b9ef9', 'word_count': 697, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa96259d-8000-5f8d-9e5c-a8c78c2b9ef9'}, {'abstract': 'In Mississippi, the historic epicenter of racist terror, a legislator says those who bring down Confederate memorials should be “lynched.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/25/opinion/confederate-memorial-mississippi-lynchings.html', 'snippet': 'In Mississippi, the historic epicenter of racist terror, a legislator says those who bring down Confederate memorials should be “lynched.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'More African-American men, women and children were hanged, burned and dismembered per capita in Mississippi between the Civil War and World War II than in any other Southern state. This bloody sacrifice to white supremacy sprang immediately to mind over the weekend when a white Mississippi state representative, Karl Oliver, railed in a Facebook post that elected officials in New Orleans deserved to be “lynched” for arranging to have four Confederate memorials removed from the city. Mr. Oliver’s grotesque remark reminds us yet again that the era of racial terror that spawned these memorials still casts a shadow over American life.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/25/opinion/25thu3web/25thu3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 405, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/25/opinion/25thu3web/25thu3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 405}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/25/opinion/25thu3web/25thu3web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 691, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/25/opinion/25thu3web/25thu3web-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1382, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/25/opinion/25thu3web/25thu3web-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/25/opinion/25thu3web/25thu3web-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/25/opinion/25thu3web/25thu3web-thumbLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'As Statues Fall, the Specter of the Noose Rises', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As Statues Fall, the Specter of the Noose Rises', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Oliver, Karl (1963- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mississippi', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Monuments and Memorials (Structures)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil Rights Movement (1954-68)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State Legislatures', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-25T07:21:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/83db15d7-3077-54ad-9c80-0ae388f62173', 'word_count': 386, 'uri': 'nyt://article/83db15d7-3077-54ad-9c80-0ae388f62173'}, {'abstract': 'Memories of the city’s race riot informed our reaction to the latest police-shooting acquittal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/25/opinion/tulsa-police-shooting-terence-crutcher.html', 'snippet': 'Memories of the city’s race riot informed our reaction to the latest police-shooting acquittal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I gathered with other black residents of Tulsa, Okla., last Wednesday night outside the county courthouse to hear a disappointing yet predictable verdict: Betty Shelby had been found not guilty of first-degree manslaughter in the police killing of Terence Crutcher.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/25/opinion/25gayleWebSub/25gayleWeb-1495661448217-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 393, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/25/opinion/25gayleWebSub/25gayleWeb-1495661448217-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 393}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/25/opinion/25gayleWebSub/25gayleWeb-1495661448217-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 671, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/25/opinion/25gayleWebSub/25gayleWeb-1495661448217-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1343, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/25/opinion/25gayleWebSub/25gayleWebSub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/25/opinion/25gayleWebSub/25gayleWebSub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/25/opinion/25gayleWebSub/25gayleWebSub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Tulsa ‘Not Guilty’ Verdict, Echoes of Historic Injustice', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police Brutality, Misconduct and Shootings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders, Attempted Murders and Homicides', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Crutcher, Terence (1976-2016)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tulsa (Okla)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-25T07:21:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Caleb Gayle', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Caleb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gayle', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/3dd057d0-bb04-5273-9617-294b1f757940', 'word_count': 864, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3dd057d0-bb04-5273-9617-294b1f757940'}, {'abstract': 'A new study challenges the myth that marriage is a magic potion.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/25/opinion/marriage-health-study.html', 'snippet': 'A new study challenges the myth that marriage is a magic potion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a nation as divided and contentious as our own, it is rare to find a belief we all share. But trust in the transformative power of marriage is close to universal — and it has endured for decades. This isn’t just a matter of faith, we’ve been assured. It’s science. Research is said to have established what our fairy tales promised: Marry and you will live happily ever after. And you will be healthier, too.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/25/opinion/25depauloWeb/25depauloWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/25/opinion/25depauloWeb/25depauloWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/25/opinion/25depauloWeb/25depauloWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/25/opinion/25depauloWeb/25depauloWeb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None,