 'The Tonight Show (TV Program)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-25T08:58:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Giovanni Russonello', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Giovanni', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Russonello', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3e8289b4-6bab-5f3c-8a8e-82bb48df8b63', 'word_count': 587, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3e8289b4-6bab-5f3c-8a8e-82bb48df8b63'}, {'abstract': 'A reality show so punishingly mundane that it becomes beautiful.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/25/magazine/letter-of-recommendation-terrace-house.html', 'snippet': 'A reality show so punishingly mundane that it becomes beautiful.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I came to reality television late, and I watched as if I were making up for lost time. I started with the “Bachelor” franchise, bingeing on old seasons when I couldn’t wait for the next one to start. Soon I moved on to “Love & Hip Hop,” then “Dating Naked” (gone but not forgotten). I took a perverse sort of pleasure in my new habit, but it wasn’t until I found “Terrace House,” a staggeringly banal Japanese franchise, that I realized the format was capable of genuine literary excellence.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/28/magazine/28lor/28mag-28lor-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/28/magazine/28lor/28mag-28lor-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/28/magazine/28lor/28mag-28lor-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/28/magazine/28lor/28mag-28lor-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1364, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/28/magazine/28lor/28mag-28lor-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/28/magazine/28lor/28mag-28lor-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/28/magazine/28lor/28mag-28lor-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Letter of Recommendation: ‘Terrace House’', 'kicker': 'Letter of Recommendation', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Terrace House’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reality Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Terrace House (TV Program)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-25T09:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Ridker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ridker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e58438c4-03da-5994-bacf-58c7e2b9f0fa', 'word_count': 883, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e58438c4-03da-5994-bacf-58c7e2b9f0fa'}, {'abstract': 'Deciphering the rise of a lifestyle guru who sells self-absorption as the ultimate luxury product.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/25/magazine/how-amanda-chantal-bacon-perfected-the-celebrity-wellness-business.html', 'snippet': 'Deciphering the rise of a lifestyle guru who sells self-absorption as the ultimate luxury product.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The amount of time I waste finding and consuming alternative-medicine supplements for “brain function” has made me at least 10 percent dumber, and that paradox is not lost on me. It was this impulse that made me pause last year at a fancy store in Brooklyn when I spotted a glass jar labeled “Brain Dust.” It had the kind of packaging that signals discreet luxury: minimalist matte label, custom type, the word “organic.” A 2.2-ounce jar cost $55. “This adaptogenic potion lights up your brain and increases mental flow,” the label said. “Neuron velocity and vision are fine tuned by toning the brain waves, in particular the alpha waves that connect to creativity.” It seemed like the kind of item that might be a prop in a comedy sketch about millennial idiots, or something a person would be duped into buying on a hidden-camera prank show.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/28/magazine/28bacon1/28bacon1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/28/magazine/28bacon1/28bacon1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/28/magazine/28bacon1/28bacon1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/28/magazine/28bacon1/28bacon1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 859, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/28/magazine/28bacon1/28bacon1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 859}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/28/magazine/28bacon1/28bacon1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 716, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/28/magazine/28bacon1/28bacon1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1431, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Amanda Chantal Bacon Perfected the Celebrity Wellness Business', 'kicker': 'Feature', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Empire of Dust', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sustainable Living', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bacon, Amanda Chantal', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Foods', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Celebrities', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Alternative and Complementary Medicine', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paltrow, Gwyneth', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-25T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Molly Young', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Molly', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Young', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/039fe489-9e2d-5045-