'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-25T10:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Schaefer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schaefer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a0ae1970-8220-5625-ab2b-ec98d722b919', 'word_count': 1115, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a0ae1970-8220-5625-ab2b-ec98d722b919'}, {'abstract': 'A bounty of television, movies and music engages with the idea of performing blackness. Jenna Wortham and Wesley Morris explore this complicated phenomenon.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/25/podcasts/being-black-in-the-age-of-wokeness.html', 'snippet': 'A bounty of television, movies and music engages with the idea of performing blackness. Jenna Wortham and Wesley Morris explore this complicated phenomenon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This month, we were out for dinner at a barbecue restaurant with a friend when a fellow customer, on his way to the bathroom, spotted us and did a sexy little dance. The soundtrack was deep-cut late-’90s R&B, and this guy was feeling it. He wanted us to know he felt it. We’re black. He’s white, and the evening went south from there. His friend, who was also white, stopped by with shots and his own dance moves. We didn’t want to dance with him and told him so. Our umbrage provoked his.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/25/magazine/25mag-still-processing-1/25mag-still-processing-1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 394, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/25/magazine/25mag-still-processing-1/25ma