tib', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/217668d9-7614-561e-9065-d257e576bc55', 'word_count': 421, 'uri': 'nyt://article/217668d9-7614-561e-9065-d257e576bc55'}, {'abstract': 'If you’ve finished watching Season 2 and are looking for some insight into its most remarkable moments, this reading list is for you.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/25/watching/master-of-none-critics-roundup.html', 'snippet': 'If you’ve finished watching Season 2 and are looking for some insight into its most remarkable moments, this reading list is for you.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From romance to pasta to beautifully framed shots of New York City at night, each season of Aziz Ansari’s Netflix dramedy “Master of None” serves up a feast for the senses. But the greatest pleasure the show offers is its innovative approach to episodic television. Whether it features a meditation on religion or a homage to a classic film, just about every half-hour of “Master of None” feels like a self-contained world, with a unique structure and visual style that complements its theme. These distinctive episodes give critics plenty to talk about, so if you’ve finished watching Season 2 and are looking for some insight into its most remarkable moments, this reading list is for you.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/12/arts/12tvcol-Master3/12tvcol-Master3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/12/arts/12tvcol-Master3/12tvcol-Master3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/12/arts/12tvcol-Master3/12tvcol-Master3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/12/arts/12tvcol-Master3/12tvcol-Master3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 337, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/12/arts/12tvcol-Master3/12tvcol-Master3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 337}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/12/arts/12tvcol-Master3/12tvcol-Master3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 574, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/12/arts/12tvcol-Master3/12tvcol-Master3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1149, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What You Should Read About the Most Interesting Episodes of ‘Master of None’ Season 2', 'kicker': 'Chatter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Netflix Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ansari, Aziz', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yang, Alan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Master of None (TV Program)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-25T16:36:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Watching', 'section_name': 'Watching', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judy Berman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Berman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b1a30c8-22c7-5e0b-8fe7-8be69dfb12de', 'word_count': 1040, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b1a30c8-22c7-5e0b-8fe7-8be69dfb12de'}, {'abstract': 'David Michôd’s film, adapted from a Michael Hastings book, puts a leader with boundless ignorance in a volatile country.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/25/movies/war-machine-netflix-brad-pitt-afghanistan.html', 'snippet': 'David Michôd’s film, adapted from a Michael Hastings book, puts a leader with boundless ignorance in a volatile country.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What does it mean to win a war? This question is never asked outright in “War Machine,” a new Netflix original movie starring Brad Pitt and written and directed by David Michôd, but it’s a substantial thematic undercarriage of this satire. The movie, which has its theatrical and TV premiere on Friday, May 26, is adapted from Michael Hastings’s 2012 book, “The Operators: The Wild and Terrifying Inside Story of America’s War in Afghanistan.” Mr. Pitt plays Gen. Glen McMahon, an arguably brilliant career soldier whose first words on touching down in Afghanistan, where he has a new mission circa 2009, are “Let’s go win this thing!” McMahon approaches the task of transforming Afghan hearts and minds with enthusiasm and boundless ignorance. The terrifying hubris depicted in the movie is ever more pertinent given that President Trump has recently signaled an intention to increase the American military presence in Afghanistan.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/26/arts/26WARMACHINEJP/26WARMACHINEJP-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/26/arts/26WARMACHINEJP/26WARMACHINEJP-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', '