udy in Contrasts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Merkel, Angela', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'North Atlantic Treaty Organization', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidents and Presidency (US)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-25T21:04:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alison Smale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alison', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a1a8d1af-dd96-5be7-a2db-7fef7fc0f60c', 'word_count': 1091, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a1a8d1af-dd96-5be7-a2db-7fef7fc0f60c'}, {'abstract': 'How the advocates of Trumpism are wrestling with its absence from the Trump presidency.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2017/05/25/letting-trump-be-trump/', 'snippet': 'How the advocates of Trumpism are wrestling with its absence from the Trump presidency.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My Sunday column was a kind of open letter to those groups of conservatives who seem likeliest to stick with Donald Trump during the rough, Comey-stirred waters that lie ahead for him. Not the Hannity-esque partisans, but the conservatives who had specifically ideological reasons for supporting his candidacy – because it promised a more populist and less plutocratic Republican Party, or a foreign policy with less piety and adventurism and more national-interested realism, or certain protections for religious conservatives in an age of secular-liberal aggression.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Letting Trump Be Trump', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2017-05-25T21:05:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/627ad671-b90b-5b5e-8491-a89ac88de1dd', 'word_count': 1915, 'uri': 'nyt://article/627ad671-b90b-5b5e-8491-a89ac88de1dd'}, {'abstract': 'Jon Brittain’s play explores the fallout when Fiona announces to her girlfriend that she wants to transition and be called Adrian.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/25/theater/rotterdam-review.html', 'snippet': 'Jon Brittain’s play explores the fallout when Fiona announces to her girlfriend that she wants to transition and be called Adrian.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Alice is in a tough spot: She’s been living with Fiona for seven years, but her parents still don’t know she’s gay. It helps that these British women have settled at a safe remove in the Dutch city of Rotterdam, but still …', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/26/arts/26ROTTERDAM1/26ROTTERDAM1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/26/arts/26ROTTERDAM1/26ROTTERDAM1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLa