'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-26T16:34:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jess Bidgood and Katharine Q. Seelye', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jess', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bidgood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Katharine', 'middlename': 'Q.', 'lastname': 'Seelye', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3750eb91-6e7c-5c7b-84ef-8a2b59d341b5', 'word_count': 1221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3750eb91-6e7c-5c7b-84ef-8a2b59d341b5'}, {'abstract': 'Scott Turow quits Illinois for The Hague in “Testimony,” and hits the best-seller list at No. 6.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/26/books/review/a-courtroom-novelist-tackles-war-crimes-in-bosnia.html', 'snippet': 'Scott Turow quits Illinois for The Hague in “Testimony,” and hits the best-seller list at No. 6.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHANGE OF VENUE: Scott Turow’s new novel, “Testimony,” enters the hardcover fiction list at No. 6, and it abandons Turow’s usual setting of Kindle County, Ill., to follow a Bosnian war-crimes trial in the International Criminal Court. Why the change? “In 2000,” Turow told me via email, “in the midst of a book tour, I attended a reception at the United States ambassador’s residence in The Hague, where I was encircled by a number of American lawyers working at war-crimes tribunals the U.N. had established. They all insisted I had to write a novel about one of those courts, because the magnitude of the cases, the diplomatic entanglements and the personal politics combined to make fertile ground. Usually, when people tell me there’s a huge injustice I must write about, it’s their divorce proceedings. But a war-crimes court seemed like a unique setting that I never forgot.” He added, “I knew far less than I should have about the sad history of the Bosnian conflict, but I soon came to see those killing fields as a provocative stage for a story about the law.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '56', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/06/04/books/review/0604-BKS-LIST/0604-BKS-LIST-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/06/04/books/review/0604-BKS-LIST/0604-BKS-LIST-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/06/04/books/review/0604-BKS-LIST/0604-BKS-LIST-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/06/04/books/review/0604-BKS-LIST/0604-BKS-LIST-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 387, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/06/04/books/review/0604-BKS-LIST/0604-BKS-LIST-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 387}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/06/04/books/review/0604-BKS-LIST/0604-BKS-LIST-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 661, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/06/04/books/review/0604-BKS-LIST/0604-BKS-LIST-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 1240, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Courtroom Novelist Tackles War Crimes in Bosnia', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, '