ultimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'No Corrections: May 29, 2017', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2017-05-28T22:26:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Corrections', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cebc6385-d327-5020-b144-d1dfd3bac6f3', 'word_count': 77, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cebc6385-d327-5020-b144-d1dfd3bac6f3'}, {'abstract': 'A growing number of companies are putting their names on areas outside their expertise, like real estate, but experts caution that missteps could taint an image.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/28/business/media/expand-brands-niches-risk.html', 'snippet': 'A growing number of companies are putting their names on areas outside their expertise, like real estate, but experts caution that missteps could taint an image.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The luxury sports carmaker Aston Martin recently unveiled plans to build Aston Martin Residences, a luxury condominium complex in Miami. Sales will start in July, with the condos scheduled to open in 2021. The car company didn’t jump into the real estate business willy-nilly, though. It is part of a gradual brand extension that began with high-end jewelry in 2012, speedboats in 2015, luxury men’s wear in 2016 and its “Art of Living” experiences, which include exotic road trips and Champagne tastings, in 2016.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/29/business/adco2/adco2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/29/business/adco2/adco2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 's