 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mariana Alessandri', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mariana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alessandri', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/c7e00a4a-bb36-5067-8ea8-5917a2666aaa', 'word_count': 1155, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c7e00a4a-bb36-5067-8ea8-5917a2666aaa'}, {'abstract': 'Remember when he said he’d keep things constitutional by donating profits from foreign guests? You didn’t believe him, did you?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/29/opinion/trump-hotel-emoluments-clause.html', 'snippet': 'Remember when he said he’d keep things constitutional by donating profits from foreign guests? You didn’t believe him, did you?', 'lead_paragraph': 'When President Trump took office, his lawyers said they had an easy way to solve the legal questions posed by his ownership of a Washington hotel. Since the Constitution’s emoluments clause bans government officials from accepting payments from foreign governments, Sheri Dillon of Morgan Lewis, standing with Mr. Trump next to a mound of files at a made-for-television news conference, said her client would “voluntarily donate all profits from foreign government payments made to his hotel to the United States Treasury.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/29/opinion/29mon2/29mon2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/29/opinion/29mon2/29mon2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/29/opinion/29mon2/29mon2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 722, 'width': 600, 's