e': 'Education Life', 'byline': {'original': 'By THE NEW YORK TIMES', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/f22e6268-24de-54c4-aa01-c2c2c44ae237', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/f22e6268-24de-54c4-aa01-c2c2c44ae237'}, {'abstract': 'Llewellyn Smith’s ‘Poisoned Water,’ on Nova on PBS, examines the crisis over the drinking supply in Flint, Mich., and the ordinary heroes who helped resolve it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/30/arts/television/review-tainted-water-bad-science-and-8000-children-exposed-to-lead.html', 'snippet': 'Llewellyn Smith’s ‘Poisoned Water,’ on Nova on PBS, examines the crisis over the drinking supply in Flint, Mich., and the ordinary heroes who helped resolve it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The resolution of the Flint, Mich., water crisis might seem like an unqualified victory for science at a time when its value is under attack. While politicians and bureaucrats denied that there was anything dangerous about the city’s discolored, foul-smelling tap water, independent testing proved that people were being exposed to toxic levels of lead. Scientific sleuthing led to indictments and a program to replace some of Flint’s antiquated water-delivery system.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/31/arts/31NOVA/31NOVA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/31/arts/31NOVA/31NOVA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/31/arts/31NOVA/31NOVA-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, '