-bf85-37c4650b3fa4-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1638, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/04/books/review/04MaslinSideBar_GrishamTips/04MaslinSideBar_GrishamTips-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/02/04/books/review/04MaslinSideBar_GrishamTips/04MaslinSideBar_GrishamTips-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/04/books/review/04MaslinSideBar_GrishamTips/04MaslinSideBar_GrishamTips-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'John Grisham’s Do’s and Don’ts for Writing Popular Fiction', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'John Grisham’s Suggestions for Writing Popular Fiction', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grisham, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-31T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Grisham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grisham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/276ee1eb-ccd4-5302-abe9-5c1e0d7ad342', 'word_count': 404, 'uri': 'nyt://article/276ee1eb-ccd4-5302-abe9-5c1e0d7ad342'}, {'abstract': 'Five new thrillers for the beach and beyond, from a very personal manhunt to a Russian spy during World War II.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/31/books/review/summer-reading-thrillers.html', 'snippet': 'Five new thrillers for the beach and beyond, from a very personal manhunt to a Russian spy during World War II.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The 2000s were thrilling here in America, in the most strictly neutral sense of the word — sensation after sensation, the fall of the towers, two wars, financial collapse, natural disaster, fear. Adults responded, very sanely one could argue, by devoting the period to reading about teenage vampires. (Or waiting on Platform 9¾ for the next train to Hogwarts.) Then things slowly stabilized, and perhaps our mood shifted too. The immense success of “Gone Girl” in 2012 seemed to consolidate that sense: The most popular novels could be about grown-ups again. Less frightened that our houses might be taken, we began to wonder instead who might hurt us while we were inside them.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/06/04/books/review/0604-BKS-Thrillers/0604-BKS-Thrillers-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/06/04/books/review/0604-BKS-Thrillers/0604-BKS-Thrillers-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/06/04/books/review/0604-BKS-Thrillers/0604-BKS-Thrillers-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/06/04/books/review/0604-BKS-Thrillers/0604-BKS-Thrillers-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 843, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/06/04/books/review/0