ame': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mindanao (Philippines)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Duterte, Rodrigo', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-31T10:30:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felipe Villamor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felipe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Villamor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d1d726f-34f9-52eb-9796-66b9ae6a59cc', 'word_count': 726, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d1d726f-34f9-52eb-9796-66b9ae6a59cc'}, {'abstract': 'Both his supporters and his opposition talk about the president as if he’s a strategic genius, despite considerable evidence to the contrary.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/31/magazine/the-enduring-appeal-of-seeing-trump-as-chess-master-in-chief.html', 'snippet': 'Both his supporters and his opposition talk about the president as if he’s a strategic genius, despite considerable evidence to the contrary.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the first months of his campaign, back when the press and his political opponents still regarded him as a curiosity or a nuisance, Donald Trump was accumulating online fans in surprising places. On the anarchic image board 4chan, he was celebrated as a fellow troll, while on Reddit, a pro-Trump community gained tens of thousands of members, shifting the entire political ecosystem on the platform. So when Trump actually began dominating the Republican primary contest, these two communities were among the first to feel a need to explain why he hadn’t been stopped — and couldn’t be.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/26/magazine/26mag-chess-trump/26mag-chess-trump-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/05/26/magazine/26mag-chess-trump/26mag-chess-trump-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/26/magazine/26mag-chess-trump/26mag-chess-trump-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/26/magazine/26mag-chess-trump/26mag-chess-trump-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/26/magazine/26mag-chess-trump/26mag-chess-trump-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/26/magazine/26mag-chess-trump/26mag-chess-trump-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/26/magazine/26mag-chess-trump/26mag-chess-trump-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1800, 'width': 1200, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Enduring Appeal of Seeing Trump as Chess Master in Chief', 'kicker': 'Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chess', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Reddit Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-05-31T11:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Herrman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Herrman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0af416f4-b7c7-554a-b607-c21373708e58', 'word_count': 1512, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0af416f4-b7c7-554a-b607-c21373708e58'}, {'abstract': 'Pollsters are leaning toward optimistic explanations that their problems of last year can be fixed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/31/upshot/a-2016-review-why-key-state-polls-were-wrong-about-trump.html', 'snippet': 'Pollsters are leaning toward optimistic explanations that their problems of last year can be fixed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nearly seven months after the presidential election, pollsters are still trying to answer a question that has rattled trust in their profession: Why did pre-election polls show Hillary Clinton leading Donald J. Trump in the battleground states that decided the presidency? Is political polling fundamentally broken? Or were the errors understandable and correctable?', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/26/upshot/30up-undchart-1496086250138/30up-undchart-1496086250138-articleLarge-v2.png', 'height': 266, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/05/26/upshot/30up-undchart-1496086250138/30up-undchart-1496086250138-articleLarge-v2.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 266}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/05/26/upshot/30up-undchart-1496086