ation': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/f77c4998-6a21-5c2b-a0ea-3830a347bcc8', 'word_count': 1149, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f77c4998-6a21-5c2b-a0ea-3830a347bcc8'}, {'abstract': 'This film re-examines Winston Churchill’s image as a gruff British bulldog of a leader by shining a light on his struggle with depression.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/31/movies/churchill-review.html', 'snippet': 'This film re-examines Winston Churchill’s image as a gruff British bulldog of a leader by shining a light on his struggle with depression.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Winston Churchill the character has appeared in dozens of films, TV movies and mini-series over the years, and the portraits always hew to the gruff British bulldog conception of this 20th-century leader. “Churchill,” a new movie directed by Jonathan Teplitzky from a script by Alex von Tunzelmann, is laudable in its effort to shine a light on the Churchill who struggled in private with crippling depression — “the black dog,” he called it. His accuracy-in-metaphor was such that sufferers of subsequent generations have frequently adopted the term.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/06/02/arts/02CHURCHILL1/02CHURCHILL1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/06/02/arts/02CHURCHILL1/02CHURCHILL1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/06/02/arts/02CHURCHILL1/02CHURCHILL1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/06/02/arts/